window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-phy-charges-fields","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Electric Charges and Fields · Practice","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Why can two electric field lines never cross each other?","image":"","options":["Because field lines carry charges of the same sign, which repel","Because at the point of intersection the electric field would have two directions, which is impossible","Because field lines always form closed loops","Because the field is zero wherever two lines meet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The tangent to a field line gives the direction of the field.\nIf two lines crossed, there would be two tangents at the crossing point. The field there would then have two directions.\nThe field at a point has a unique direction, so crossing is ruled out.\nField lines are not charged objects, so option (1) is wrong. Electrostatic lines also do not form closed loops, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the physical significance of the electric field are correct, according to NCERT?\n(i) In electrostatics, the concept of electric field is convenient but not really necessary.\n(ii) When a charge $q_1$ is accelerated, the force on a distant charge $q_2$ changes instantaneously.\n(iii) Electromagnetic fields can transport energy, even after their source has been switched off.\n(iv) The concept of field was first introduced by Faraday.\n(v) Electric fields are regarded merely as mathematical constructs, because they are detected only through forces on charges.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in electrostatics, forces can be found directly from Coulomb's law and superposition.\n(ii) Incorrect: signals travel at most at speed c, so the effect on $q_2$ arrives after a time delay.\n(iii) Correct: propagating EM fields carry energy even after the source is switched off.\n(iv) Correct: Faraday introduced the field concept.\n(v) Incorrect: fields are regarded as physical entities with their own dynamics.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Gauss's law are correct?\n(a) It holds for any closed surface, whatever its shape or size.\n(b) The field $\\vec{E}$ used to calculate the flux is produced only by the charges enclosed by the surface.\n(c) The charge q in the law is the algebraic sum of all enclosed charges, wherever they lie inside the surface.\n(d) A Gaussian surface may pass through a continuous charge distribution.\n(e) If the net flux through a closed surface is zero, the electric field must be zero at every point of the surface.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: Gauss's law holds for any closed surface.\n(b) Incorrect: $\\vec{E}$ is due to all charges, inside and outside; only q counts the enclosed charge.\n(c) Correct: q is the sum of all enclosed charges, located anywhere inside.\n(d) Correct: only discrete charges must be avoided.\n(e) Incorrect: a closed cylinder in a uniform field has zero net flux even though E is non-zero on it.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ratio of the electrostatic force to the gravitational force between two protons is the same whether they are $10^{-15}$ m apart or 1 m apart.\nReason (R): Both Coulomb's law and Newton's law of gravitation vary inversely as the square of the separation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $\\frac{F_e}{F_G} = \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 G m_p^2} \\approx 1.3 \\times 10^{36}$, with no r in the expression.\nR is true: both forces depend on distance as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$.\nR explains A, because the identical $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ factors cancel in the ratio. The nature differs, though: gravity is attractive and the Coulomb force between protons is repulsive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the electric field are correct?\n(i) If the test charge used to measure the field due to Q is doubled, the field at that point doubles.\n(ii) The SI unit of electric field is N/C.\n(iii) At all points equidistant from an isolated point charge, its electric field has the same magnitude.\n(iv) The electric field of a charge exists at a point only when a test charge is placed there.\n(v) The field at a point due to a system of charges is the vector sum of the fields due to the individual charges.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: F is proportional to q, so F/q, and hence E, does not change.\n(ii) Correct: this follows from F = qE.\n(iii) Correct: E depends only on r, which gives spherical symmetry.\n(iv) Incorrect: the field exists at every point in space whether or not a test charge is present.\n(v) Correct: this is the superposition principle.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A small flat area element of $2\\ \\text{cm}^2$ is placed perpendicular to the radial field lines of a point charge, at a distance of 20 cm from the charge. What is the solid angle it subtends at the charge?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ sr","$5.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ sr","$1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ sr","$5.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ sr"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta S = 2\\ \\text{cm}^2 = 2 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\text{m}^2$, r = 0.20 m.\nFormula: $\\Delta \\Omega = \\frac{\\Delta S}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta \\Omega = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.04}$\nSo $\\Delta \\Omega = 5.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ sr.\nUsing $\\frac{\\Delta S}{r}$ instead, as for a plane angle, gives the wrong value $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Charged bodies can be treated as point charges when their sizes are very small compared with the distances between them","Charge is a scalar, and the total charge of a system is the algebraic sum of the individual charges","Like mass, the charge of a body is always a positive quantity","When a neutron turns into a proton and an electron, the total charge is zero before and after the change"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: point charges are an idealisation valid when size is much smaller than separation.\n(2) Correct: charges add like real numbers (additivity).\n(3) Incorrect: mass is always positive, but charge can be positive or negative. This is the key difference between mass and charge.\n(4) Correct: the proton (+e) and electron (−e) give zero total charge, so charge is conserved.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following classifications agrees with NCERT?","image":"","options":["Earth – insulator","Porcelain – conductor","Semiconductor – resistance to the movement of charges intermediate between that of conductors and insulators","Nylon – conductor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Metals, human and animal bodies and the earth are conductors, so option (1) is wrong.\nGlass, porcelain, plastic, nylon and wood are insulators, so options (2) and (4) are wrong.\nSemiconductors form a third category. Their resistance to the movement of charges lies between that of conductors and insulators.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A rod X, electrified by rubbing, repels a glass rod that has been rubbed with silk. Which conclusion about X is correct?","image":"","options":["X is negatively charged and will repel the silk used to rub the glass rod","X has gained electrons and will attract a plastic rod rubbed with fur","X is positively charged and will attract the cat's fur used to rub a plastic rod","X has lost electrons and will repel the cat's fur used to rub a plastic rod"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Repulsion happens only between like charges. Glass rubbed with silk is positive, so X is positive.\nA positive body has lost electrons, which rules out options (1) and (2).\nCat's fur is positive by convention, so X repels the fur. Option (3) is wrong because it says X attracts the fur.\nX would attract a negative plastic rod or silk, but it repels the fur.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A thin spherical shell of radius R carries a total charge q spread uniformly over its surface. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point inside the shell at distance r (< R) from its centre?","image":"","options":["Zero","$\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$","$\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$","$\\frac{qr}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take a spherical Gaussian surface of radius r < R, concentric with the shell. It encloses no charge.\nBy symmetry, E has the same magnitude everywhere on this surface, so $E\\times4\\pi r^2 = 0$, giving $E = 0$.\nThe option $\\frac{qr}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$ is the field inside a uniformly charged solid sphere, not a thin shell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth and becomes positively charged. What is the correct microscopic explanation?","image":"","options":["Protons move from the silk to the glass rod","Electrons move from the glass rod to the silk","Positive charge is created on the rod by friction","Electrons move from the silk to the glass rod"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In solids, only the loosely bound electrons are transferred from one body to another.\nOn rubbing, some electrons leave the glass rod and go to the silk. The rod is left with a deficit of electrons, so it is positive, and the silk becomes negative.\nProtons do not move, and friction creates no new charge.\nElectrons moving onto the rod would make it negative, not positive.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Ratio $\\frac{F_e}{F_G}$ for an electron and a proton\n(B) Ratio $\\frac{F_e}{F_G}$ for two protons\n(C) Permittivity of free space $\\varepsilon_0$\n(D) Constant $k = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$\nList-II:\n(i) $8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$\n(ii) $1.3 \\times 10^{36}$\n(iii) $9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$\n(iv) $2.4 \\times 10^{39}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Electron–proton: $\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 G m_p m_e} \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{39}$, so A matches (iv).\n(B) Proton–proton: $m_e$ is replaced by the larger $m_p$, so the ratio is smaller, about $1.3 \\times 10^{36}$. B matches (ii).\n(C) $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$, so C matches (i).\n(D) $k \\approx 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+2\\ \\mu C$, $+2\\ \\mu C$ and $-2\\ \\mu C$ are placed at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side 0.3 m. What is the magnitude of the net force on the negative charge? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^9\\ N\\,m^2C^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["0.69 N","0.40 N","0.80 N","0.35 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 2\\ \\mu C$ and $l = 0.3$ m.\nFormula: each pair force is $F = \\frac{kq^2}{l^2}$. The two attractions on $-q$ make $60^\\circ$ with each other, so $F_{net} = 2F\\cos 30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F$.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{9\\times10^9 \\times 4\\times10^{-12}}{0.09} = 0.4$ N.\nSo $F_{net} = \\sqrt{3} \\times 0.4 \\approx 0.69$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about electrostatic field lines are correct?\n(i) They start from positive charges and end at negative charges.\n(ii) Two field lines may cross at a point where the field is very strong.\n(iii) Electrostatic field lines can form closed loops.\n(iv) In a charge-free region they are continuous curves without breaks.\n(v) The relative closeness of field lines indicates the relative strength of the field.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: field lines start at positive charges and end at negative charges, or at infinity if there is a single charge.\n(ii) Incorrect: if two lines crossed, the field would have two directions at that point, which is absurd. So field lines never cross.\n(iii) Incorrect: the electrostatic field is conservative, so its field lines cannot form closed loops.\n(iv) Correct.\n(v) Correct: lines crowd together where the field is strong.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+2$ nC and $-2$ nC are placed 2 cm apart. Using the exact expression for the field on the dipole axis, find the magnitude of the electric field at a point on the axis 3 cm from the centre of the dipole, on the side of the positive charge. (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9 \\times 10^9$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$2.67 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$3.38 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$1.69 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$6.75 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 2 \\times 10^{-9}$ C, $a = 0.01$ m, $r = 0.03$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{4qar}{(r^2-a^2)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9 \\times 10^9 \\times 4 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 0.01 \\times 0.03}{(8 \\times 10^{-4})^2} = \\frac{0.0216}{6.4 \\times 10^{-7}}$.\nSo $E \\approx 3.38 \\times 10^4$ N/C.\nThe short-dipole formula $\\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ gives $2.67 \\times 10^4$ N/C. It fails here because $r$ is not much larger than $a$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two free point charges $+1\\ \\mu C$ and $+9\\ \\mu C$ are 0.4 m apart. A third charge $Q$ is placed on the line joining them so that all three charges are in equilibrium. Find $Q$ (in $\\mu C$).","image":"","options":["$-0.5625\\ \\mu C$","$-0.25\\ \\mu C$","$-0.75\\ \\mu C$","$-0.10\\ \\mu C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: For $Q$ to be in equilibrium, it must lie between the charges at distance $x$ from $+1\\ \\mu C$, with $\\frac{1}{x^2} = \\frac{9}{(0.4-x)^2}$. This gives $0.4 - x = 3x$, so $x = 0.1$ m.\nStep 2: For $+1\\ \\mu C$ to be in equilibrium, the pull of $Q$ must balance the push of $+9\\ \\mu C$: $\\frac{|Q|}{(0.1)^2} = \\frac{9}{(0.4)^2}$.\nSo $|Q| = \\frac{9 \\times 0.01}{0.16} = 0.5625\\ \\mu C$. $Q$ must be negative to attract both charges.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+2\\,\\mu C$, $+2\\,\\mu C$ and $-2\\,\\mu C$ are placed at the vertices A, B and C of an equilateral triangle of side 0.3 m. What is the magnitude of the net force on the charge at C? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^9$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["0.40 N","0.80 N","0.69 N","0.20 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $|q| = 2\\,\\mu C$, $l = 0.3$ m.\nFormula: Each pair interacts with $F = \\frac{kq^2}{l^2}$. The charge at C is attracted towards both A and B along CA and CB. These two forces make an angle of $60^\\circ$ with each other.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{9\\times10^9 \\times 4\\times10^{-12}}{0.09} = 0.4$ N. The resultant is $2F\\cos 30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F$.\nSo $F_C = \\sqrt{3} \\times 0.4 \\approx 0.69$ N, along the bisector of angle C.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron and a proton are placed, one at a time, in the same uniform electric field. What is the ratio of the magnitude of the electron's acceleration to that of the proton's? ($m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg, $m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg)","image":"","options":["1833","42.8","1","$5.46 \\times 10^{-4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: both particles have charge of magnitude $e$ and feel a force of the same magnitude, $eE$.\nFormula: $a = \\frac{eE}{m}$, so $\\frac{a_e}{a_p} = \\frac{m_p}{m_e}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}} \\approx 1833$.\nThe value 42.8 is the square root of this ratio. That square root gives the ratio of fall times, not accelerations.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C and $-2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C are placed at two corners of an equilateral triangle of side 0.2 m. What is the magnitude of the resultant electric field at the third corner? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["9000 N/C","4500 N/C","7794 N/C","0 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $|q| = 2\\times10^{-8}$ C, and the distance from each charge is 0.2 m.\nEach field: $E_1 = E_2 = \\frac{9\\times10^{9}\\times2\\times10^{-8}}{(0.2)^2} = 4500$ N/C.\nThe field of +q points away from +q, and the field of −q points towards −q. The angle between these two field vectors is 120°.\nComponents perpendicular to the line joining the charges cancel. Components parallel to that line add: $E = 2 \\times 4500 \\cos 60^\\circ = 4500$ N/C.\nThe resultant points parallel to the direction from +q to −q.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Electric flux through a small area element is defined as $\\Delta\\phi = \\vec{E}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{S}$. What is the SI unit of electric flux?","image":"","options":["N C$^{-1}$ m$^{2}$","N C m$^{-2}$","N m$^{2}$ C$^{-2}$","C$^{2}$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Electric field has the unit N C$^{-1}$ and area has the unit m$^{2}$.\nFlux is $E\\Delta S\\cos\\theta$, and $\\cos\\theta$ has no unit, so the unit of flux is N C$^{-1}$ m$^{2}$.\nN m$^{2}$ C$^{-2}$ is the unit of $k$ in Coulomb's law.\nC$^{2}$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$ is the unit of $\\varepsilon_0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Quantisation of charge was first suggested by Faraday's experimental laws of electrolysis.\n(ii) Millikan experimentally demonstrated quantisation of charge in 1912.\n(iii) A body can carry a free charge of $2.4 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\n(iv) For macroscopic charges of the order of $\\mu C$, quantisation of charge has no practical consequence.\n(v) Like charge, the mass of a body can also be positive or negative.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Faraday's laws of electrolysis first suggested quantisation.\n(ii) Correct: Millikan demonstrated it in 1912.\n(iii) Incorrect: $\\frac{2.4 \\times 10^{-19}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = 1.5$, which is not an integer.\n(iv) Correct: a $\\mu C$ contains about $10^{13}e$, so charge appears continuous.\n(v) Incorrect: mass is always positive; only charge can be negative.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Coulomb's law in vector form is $\\vec{F}_{21} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r_{21}^2} \\hat{r}_{21}$, where $\\vec{r}_{21} = \\vec{r}_2 - \\vec{r}_1$. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\vec{F}_{21}$ is the force on $q_1$ due to $q_2$","$\\hat{r}_{21}$ points from $q_2$ towards $q_1$","If $q_1$ and $q_2$ have opposite signs, $\\vec{F}_{21}$ points along $\\hat{r}_{12}$","Separate equations are needed for like and unlike charges"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $\\vec{F}_{21}$ is the force on $q_2$ due to $q_1$.\n(2) Incorrect: $\\vec{r}_{21} = \\vec{r}_2 - \\vec{r}_1$ points from 1 to 2.\n(3) Correct: if $q_1 q_2 < 0$, the force is along $-\\hat{r}_{21} = \\hat{r}_{12}$, i.e. towards $q_1$. This is attraction.\n(4) Incorrect: the sign of $q_1 q_2$ handles both cases automatically.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A point charge of $4 \\times 10^{-9}$ C is placed in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 0.2 m from it? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["180 N/C","450 N/C","900 N/C","9000 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $Q = 4 \\times 10^{-9}$ C and $r = 0.2$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{Q}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^{9} \\times 4\\times10^{-9}}{(0.2)^2} = \\frac{36}{0.04}$\nSo E = 900 N/C.\nThe value 180 N/C comes from dividing by r instead of $r^2$, which is a common slip.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A plastic comb run through dry hair attracts small bits of uncharged paper.\nReason (R): A dipole placed in a uniform electric field experiences a net force in the direction of the field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The charged comb polarises the paper, inducing a dipole moment along its field.\nThe comb's field is non-uniform, so this induced dipole feels a net force towards the comb.\nR is false. In a uniform field the forces $q\\vec{E}$ and $-q\\vec{E}$ cancel, so the net force on a dipole is zero.\nA net force arises only because the field is non-uniform.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two small identical metal spheres A and B carry charges and repel each other with a force of 0.8 N when their centres are a distance r apart. Sphere A is touched once by an identical uncharged sphere. Sphere B is touched one after another by two separate identical uncharged spheres. The separation between A and B is then made $\\frac{r}{2}$. What is the new force between A and B? (Sizes are negligible compared with the separation.)","image":"","options":["0.8 N","0.2 N","1.6 N","0.4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: initially $F = \\frac{kqq'}{r^2} = 0.8$ N.\nCharge on A after one touch: $\\frac{q}{2}$. Charge on B after two successive touches: $\\frac{q'}{2}$, then $\\frac{q'}{4}$.\nFormula: $F' = \\frac{k(q/2)(q'/4)}{(r/2)^2} = \\frac{1}{8} \\times 4 \\times \\frac{kqq'}{r^2} = \\frac{F}{2}$\nSo $F' = 0.4$ N.\nIf B were touched only once, as in the NCERT example, the force would stay at 0.8 N.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two thin concentric spherical shells have radii 10 cm and 20 cm. The inner shell carries $+3\\ \\mu C$ and the outer shell carries $-6\\ \\mu C$, each spread uniformly. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 15 cm from the common centre? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.2\\times10^{6}$ N/C","$3.6\\times10^{6}$ N/C","$2.7\\times10^{6}$ N/C","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the point is at r = 0.15 m, which lies outside the inner shell but inside the outer shell.\nThe outer shell gives zero field at points inside it. The inner shell acts as a point charge of $3\\ \\mu C$ at the centre.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{kq}{r^2} = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times3\\times10^{-6}}{(0.15)^2}$.\nSo $E = 1.2\\times10^6$ N/C, directed radially outward.\nThe option $2.7\\times10^6$ N/C wrongly uses r = 0.1 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"The relation $\\vec{F} = q\\vec{E}$ is used to define the SI unit of electric field. That unit is:","image":"","options":["C/N","N/C","N m$^2$/C","N C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"From $\\vec{F} = q\\vec{E}$, we get $E = F/q$.\nSo the unit of E is newton per coulomb, N/C. This is the force on a unit positive charge.\nC/N is the inverse of this. N m$^2$/C is the unit of electric flux.\nNCERT mentions V/m as an alternate unit, introduced in the next chapter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At distances much larger than its size, the electric field of an electric dipole falls off as $\\frac{1}{r^3}$ rather than $\\frac{1}{r^2}$.\nReason (R): At points on the equatorial plane of a dipole, the electric field is directed along the dipole moment $\\vec{p}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. For $r \\gg a$, the axial field is $\\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ and the equatorial field is $\\frac{p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$. Both vary as $1/r^3$ because the fields of +q and –q nearly cancel far away.\nR is false. On the equatorial plane, $\\vec{E} = -\\frac{\\vec{p}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$, so the field points opposite to $\\vec{p}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges $+4\\ \\mu C$ and $-4\\ \\mu C$ are held 3 mm apart. What is the magnitude of the dipole moment of this pair?","image":"","options":["$6.0 \\times 10^{-9}$ C m","$1.2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C m","$2.4 \\times 10^{-8}$ C m","$1.2 \\times 10^{-5}$ C m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 4 \\times 10^{-6}$ C and separation $2a = 3 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: $p = q \\times 2a$. The separation used is the full distance between the charges, not the half-distance $a$.\nSubstituting: $p = 4 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 3 \\times 10^{-3} = 1.2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C m.\nOption (1) uses only $a$, and option (3) doubles the separation again.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Three equal charges $q$ sit at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side $l$. A charge $Q$ of the same sign as $q$ is placed at the centroid. The net electrostatic force on $Q$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ along one median","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ along one median","zero","$\\frac{9Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ along one median"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each vertex is at distance $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{3}}$ from the centroid, so each force has magnitude $\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$.\nThe three forces are equal in size and $120^\\circ$ apart.\nThe resultant of any two is equal and opposite to the third, so the net force is zero.\nSymmetry agrees: rotating the system by $120^\\circ$ leaves it unchanged, so no direction can be preferred.\nOption (1) is the size of a single force, not the vector sum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges of $+2\\,\\mu$C and $+3\\,\\mu$C are held 30 cm apart in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the force between them? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["0.06 N","1.8 N","0.6 N","0.2 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $q_1 = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ C, $q_2 = 3 \\times 10^{-6}$ C, $r = 0.30$ m.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 6 \\times 10^{-12}}{0.09} = \\frac{0.054}{0.09}$\nSo $F = 0.6$ N. The force is repulsive because both charges are positive.\nIf you forget to square r, you get the wrong value of 0.18 N.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Consider charged particles released from rest in a uniform electric field $E$, with gravity neglected. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The force on an electron is opposite to the direction of $E$.\n(ii) A proton moves with constant acceleration.\n(iii) The time to cover a distance $h$ is independent of the particle's mass, just as in free fall under gravity.\n(iv) For given $h$ and $E$, the time of fall of a particle of charge $e$ is proportional to $\\sqrt{m}$.\n(v) If $E$ points vertically upward, an electron released from rest moves upward.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a negative charge feels a force of $-eE$, opposite to the field.\n(ii) Correct: a uniform $E$ gives a constant force and so a constant acceleration $\\frac{eE}{m}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm}{eE}}$ depends on mass. Mass-independence holds only under gravity.\n(iv) Correct: this follows directly from the same formula.\n(v) Incorrect: the force on the electron is downward, so it moves downward.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A thin spherical shell of radius 10 cm carries a uniformly distributed charge of $+3$ nC. A point charge of $-1$ nC is fixed at its centre. What is the ratio of the magnitude of the electric field at 5 cm from the centre to that at 20 cm from the centre?","image":"","options":["2","4","8","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: shell charge = +3 nC, central charge = $-1$ nC, R = 10 cm.\nAt r = 5 cm (inside the shell): the shell's own field is zero, so only the $-1$ nC charge contributes. $E_1 = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times10^{-9}}{(0.05)^2} = 3600$ N/C.\nAt r = 20 cm (outside): by Gauss's law the enclosed charge is $+3 - 1 = +2$ nC. $E_2 = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times2\\times10^{-9}}{(0.2)^2} = 450$ N/C.\nSo the ratio is $\\frac{3600}{450} = 8$.\nThe tempting answer 16 assumes the same charge acts at both points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An isolated electron and proton attract each other electrically. Take $m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg and $m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg, and neglect gravity. What is the ratio of the magnitude of the electron's acceleration to that of the proton's acceleration?","image":"","options":["1","1833","$5.46 \\times 10^{-4}$","$2.4 \\times 10^{39}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By Newton's third law, which Coulomb's law obeys, both particles feel forces of equal magnitude F.\nFormula: $a = \\frac{F}{m}$, so $\\frac{a_e}{a_p} = \\frac{m_p}{m_e}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}}$\nSo the ratio is about 1833. The lighter electron accelerates far more. For example, at 1 Å, $a_e = 2.5 \\times 10^{22}$ m s$^{-2}$ and $a_p = 1.4 \\times 10^{19}$ m s$^{-2}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A very long straight thin wire carries a uniform linear charge density of $2\\ \\mu C/m$. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 10 cm from the wire, away from its ends? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$3.6\\times10^{5}$ N/C","$1.8\\times10^{5}$ N/C","$3.6\\times10^{6}$ N/C","$7.2\\times10^{5}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 2\\times10^{-6}$ C/m and $r = 0.1$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r} = \\frac{2k\\lambda}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{2\\times9\\times10^9\\times2\\times10^{-6}}{0.1}$.\nSo $E = 3.6\\times10^5$ N/C, directed radially outward.\nThe option $1.8\\times10^5$ N/C comes from wrongly using $k\\lambda/r$ instead of $2k\\lambda/r$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field is $\\vec{E} = 300\\,\\hat{i}$ N/C for x > 0 and $\\vec{E} = -300\\,\\hat{i}$ N/C for x < 0. A right circular cylinder of length 30 cm and radius 4 cm has its axis along the x-axis and its centre at the origin. What is the net charge enclosed by the cylinder? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$2.67\\times10^{-11}$ C","$1.34\\times10^{-11}$ C","Zero","$5.34\\times10^{-11}$ C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: E = 300 N/C pointing away from the yz-plane on both sides; each flat face has area $\\pi(0.04)^2$ m$^2$.\nOn each flat face, $\\vec{E}$ is along the outward normal, so each face has flux $300\\times\\pi(0.04)^2 \\approx 1.508$ N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$. The curved surface has zero flux.\nNet flux $\\phi \\approx 3.016$ N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$.\nBy Gauss's law, $q = \\varepsilon_0\\phi = 3.016\\times8.854\\times10^{-12} \\approx 2.67\\times10^{-11}$ C.\nThe value $1.34\\times10^{-11}$ C counts only one face.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A thin spherical shell of radius 10 cm carries a charge of 4 nC spread uniformly over its surface. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 20 cm from the centre of the shell? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N $m^2$ $C^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["900 N/C","3600 N/C","180 N/C","0 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 4\\times10^{-9}$ C, $R = 0.1$ m and $r = 0.2$ m. The point is outside the shell because $r > R$.\nFormula: outside the shell, the field is the same as if all the charge sat at the centre, so $E = \\frac{kq}{r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times4\\times10^{-9}}{(0.2)^2} = \\frac{36}{0.04}$.\nSo $E = 900$ N/C.\nThe value 3600 N/C wrongly uses $R$ in place of $r$. The value 0 applies only to points inside the shell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Equal charges $q$ are at vertices B and C of an equilateral triangle ABC of side $l$, and vertex A is empty. A charge $Q$ of the same sign is placed at the centroid O. The net force on $Q$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ directed from O towards A","$\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ directed from A towards O","$\\frac{Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$ directed from O towards A","zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: OB = OC = $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so each force is $\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$, pointing along BO and CO (repulsion).\nThe angle between these two forces is $120^\\circ$.\nTheir resultant has the same magnitude, $\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$, and points along OA, i.e. towards the empty vertex.\nThis matches Example 1.5, where this resultant exactly cancels the force from the charge at A.\nOption (3) forgets that the distance is $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{3}}$, not $l$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"For a short dipole, the electric field at a point on its equatorial plane at distance r from the centre has magnitude E. What is the field magnitude at a point on its axis at distance 2r from the centre? (Take $r \\gg a$.)","image":"","options":["$E/2$","$E/4$","$E/8$","$2E$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: equatorial field at distance r is $E = \\frac{p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^3}$.\nFormula: the axial field at distance $x$ is $\\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 x^3}$.\nSubstituting $x = 2r$: $E_{axial} = \\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 (8r^3)} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^3}$.\nSo $E_{axial} = E/4$.\nOption (3) forgets the factor 2 that makes the axial field larger.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity) with List-II (SI unit):\nList-I\n(A) Surface charge density\n(B) Linear charge density\n(C) Electric flux\n(D) Electric field\nList-II\n(I) $N\\,C^{-1}$\n(II) $C\\,m^{-1}$\n(III) $N\\,C^{-1}\\,m^2$\n(IV) $C\\,m^{-2}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) $\\sigma = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta S}$, so its unit is $C\\,m^{-2}$ (IV).\n(B) $\\lambda = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta l}$, so its unit is $C\\,m^{-1}$ (II).\n(C) Flux is $\\vec{E}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{S}$, so its unit is $N\\,C^{-1}\\,m^2$ (III).\n(D) $E = \\frac{F}{q}$, so its unit is $N\\,C^{-1}$ (I).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Starting from rest, a proton takes a longer time than an electron to fall through the same distance in a uniform electric field of the same magnitude. (The field direction is chosen so that each particle is pushed downward.)\nReason (R): Both particles experience forces of equal magnitude $eE$, so their accelerations are inversely proportional to their masses.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: the charges have equal magnitude, so the force magnitude $eE$ is the same for both. Then $a = \\frac{eE}{m}$, which is inversely proportional to mass.\nA is true: since $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm}{eE}}$, the heavier proton has the smaller acceleration and takes longer. The time scales as $\\sqrt{m}$, so it is about 43 times longer.\nThe smaller acceleration in R is exactly why the proton is slower, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electric field line is drawn through a region containing several charges. At any point on the line, the tangent to the curve gives:","image":"","options":["the direction of the force on a negative test charge placed there","the direction of the net electric field at that point","the direction of the field due to the nearest charge only","the direction in which the field strength decreases fastest"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a field line as a curve drawn so that its tangent at each point is along the net electric field at that point.\nAn arrow on the curve picks one of the two directions the tangent allows.\nThe force on a negative charge is opposite to the field, so option (1) fails.\nThe tangent gives the net field of all the charges, not the field of the nearest charge alone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electric dipole of moment $2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C m is placed in a uniform electric field of $5 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C. Its dipole moment makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the field. What is the magnitude of the torque on the dipole?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ N m","$8.7 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m","$5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m","$2.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $p = 2 \\times 10^{-8}$ C m, $E = 5 \\times 10^4$ N/C, $\\theta = 30^\\circ$.\nFormula: $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}$, so the magnitude is $\\tau = pE\\sin\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $\\tau = 2 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 5 \\times 10^4 \\times 0.5$.\nSo $\\tau = 5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m.\nOption (2) wrongly uses $\\cos\\theta$ instead of $\\sin\\theta$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Three point charges $q_1$, $q_2$ and $q_3$ are held fixed in vacuum. Which of the following statements about the force on $q_1$ is correct?","image":"","options":["The force on $q_1$ due to $q_2$ changes when $q_3$ is brought nearby, because $q_3$ disturbs the interaction between $q_1$ and $q_2$.","The total force on $q_1$ is the algebraic (scalar) sum of the magnitudes of the forces due to $q_2$ and $q_3$.","The force on $q_1$ due to $q_2$ is given by Coulomb's law exactly as if $q_3$ were absent, and the total force is the vector sum of the individual forces.","The superposition principle applies only when all the charges have the same sign."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT's principle of superposition states that the force between any pair of charges is unaffected by the presence of other charges.\nThe total force on $q_1$ is the vector sum $\\vec{F}_{12} + \\vec{F}_{13}$, found using the parallelogram law.\nOption (1) is wrong because the pairwise forces are independent of the other charges.\nOption (2) is wrong because forces are vectors, so their directions matter. Simply adding magnitudes is incorrect.\nOption (4) is wrong because the principle holds for charges of any sign.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Electrons leave a body and reach another body at a steady rate of $2.5 \\times 10^{10}$ electrons per second. Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. How long will it take for the second body to collect a charge of magnitude 0.4 C?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ s","$2.5 \\times 10^{9}$ s","$6.4 \\times 10^{7}$ s","$4.0 \\times 10^{9}$ s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $2.5 \\times 10^{10}$ electrons per second, $q = 0.4$ C, $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nCharge collected per second: $2.5 \\times 10^{10} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 4 \\times 10^{-9}$ C/s.\nFormula: $t = \\frac{q}{\\text{charge per second}}$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{0.4}{4 \\times 10^{-9}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ s, which is about 3.2 years.\nSo even 0.4 C takes years to collect, showing that 1 C is a very large unit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A Gaussian surface should not be chosen so that it passes through a discrete point charge.\nReason (R): The electric field due to a system of discrete charges is not well defined at the location of any of those charges.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT advises that a Gaussian surface must not pass through any discrete charge.\nR is true. As you approach a point charge, the field grows without bound, so E has no definite value at that point.\nR explains A: the flux $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{S}$ cannot be evaluated where E is undefined.\nA Gaussian surface may, however, pass through a continuous charge distribution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In an early atomic model, a point nucleus of charge $+Ze$ is surrounded by a uniform spherical distribution of negative charge of radius $R$, so that the atom is neutral. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The electric field is zero at every point with $r > R$.\n(ii) For $r < R$, the field is directed radially outward.\n(iii) At $r = R$, the field has magnitude $\\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$.\n(iv) As $r \\to 0$, the field magnitude grows without bound.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gauss's law gives $E(r) = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2} - \\frac{r}{R^3}\\right)$ for $r < R$.\n(i) Correct: the enclosed charge is zero for $r > R$.\n(ii) Correct: for $r < R$, $\\frac{1}{r^2} > \\frac{r}{R^3}$, so the enclosed charge is positive and $E$ points outward.\n(iii) Incorrect: substituting $r = R$ gives $E = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: the $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ term dominates near the nucleus.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a gold-leaf electroscope, a charged object touches the metal knob at the top. What indicates the amount of charge on the electroscope?","image":"","options":["The colour change of the gold leaves","The degree of divergence of the gold leaves","The rise in temperature of the metal rod","Whether the leaves attract the walls of the box"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Charge flows from the object through the metal rod to the two gold leaves.\nBoth leaves now carry like charge, so they repel each other and spread apart.\nMore charge gives a larger repulsion and therefore wider divergence. The degree of divergence indicates the amount of charge.\nThe other options are not the working principle of the instrument.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A Gaussian surface encloses a charge $q_1$. Another charge $q_2$ lies outside the surface.\nStatement I: The electric field E appearing in the flux integral on the left side of Gauss's law is produced only by $q_1$.\nStatement II: The charge appearing on the right side of Gauss's law is only $q_1$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The field at each point of the Gaussian surface is produced by all charges, both inside and outside. Here that means both $q_1$ and $q_2$.\nStatement II is correct. The right side, $q/\\varepsilon_0$, contains only the total charge enclosed by the surface.\nThe field of $q_2$ contributes zero net flux through the closed surface, even though it changes E at individual points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Source charge\n(b) Test charge\n(c) $\\lim_{q\\to0} \\frac{\\vec{F}}{q}$\n(d) Field due to an isolated point charge\nList-II:\n(i) Magnitude varies as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ and is spherically symmetric\n(ii) Charge that produces the field and must stay at its location\n(iii) Very small charge used to probe the effect of the field\n(iv) Force per unit positive charge, independent of the probing charge\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The source charge Q produces the field and must remain at its original location, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) The test charge q is made very small and tests the effect of the source, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) The limit of F/q defines E, the force per unit positive charge, which does not depend on q, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) A point charge gives $E \\propto 1/r^2$ with the same magnitude on any sphere around it, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron and a proton are held 2 Å ($2 \\times 10^{-10}$ m) apart in vacuum and then released. What is the magnitude of the initial acceleration of the electron due to their mutual electrostatic attraction? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$, $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg, $m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg.)","image":"","options":["$2.5 \\times 10^{22}$ m/s$^2$","$6.3 \\times 10^{21}$ m/s$^2$","$3.4 \\times 10^{18}$ m/s$^2$","$1.3 \\times 10^{22}$ m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 2 \\times 10^{-10}$ m, $q = e$ for each particle.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{ke^2}{r^2}$ and $a_e = \\frac{F}{m_e}$\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 2.56 \\times 10^{-38}}{4 \\times 10^{-20}} = 5.76 \\times 10^{-9}$ N\n$a_e = \\frac{5.76 \\times 10^{-9}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}} \\approx 6.3 \\times 10^{21}$ m/s$^2$\nThe value $3.4 \\times 10^{18}$ m/s$^2$ is the proton's acceleration (same force, larger mass). The value $2.5 \\times 10^{22}$ m/s$^2$ is the answer for a 1 Å separation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a free neutron turns into a proton and an electron, the total charge before and after the process is the same.\nReason (R): Charge-carrying particles can never be created or destroyed in any process.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The neutron has zero charge, and the proton ($+e$) and electron ($-e$) together also give zero. The total charge is conserved.\nR is false. NCERT states that charge-carrying particles may be created or destroyed. The proton and electron are created in this very process.\nWhat cannot change is the net charge of an isolated system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A small metal sphere carries charge $q$. It is touched to an identical uncharged sphere, which is then removed. This is repeated two more times, each time with a fresh identical uncharged sphere. This is the method Coulomb used to obtain known fractions of charge. What charge is finally left on the original sphere?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{q}{3}$","$\\frac{q}{6}$","$\\frac{q}{4}$","$\\frac{q}{8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"When two identical spheres touch, symmetry makes the charge divide equally, so each contact halves the charge.\nAfter 1st contact: $\\frac{q}{2}$. After 2nd: $\\frac{q}{4}$. After 3rd: $\\frac{q}{8}$.\nThe charge halves each time; it does not decrease by a fixed amount. So $\\frac{q}{3}$ and $\\frac{q}{6}$ are wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match each situation of a permanent dipole $\\vec{p}$ in List-I with the correct description of the net force and torque on it in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to a uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(B) $\\vec{p}$ perpendicular to a uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(C) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(D) $\\vec{p}$ antiparallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$\nList-II\n(1) Zero net force, zero torque\n(2) Zero net force, torque of magnitude $pE$\n(3) Net force towards increasing field, zero torque\n(4) Net force towards decreasing field, zero torque","image":"","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) In a uniform field the forces $q\\vec{E}$ and $-q\\vec{E}$ cancel. Since $\\vec{p}$ is parallel to $\\vec{E}$, $\\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E} = 0$. So A-1.\n(B) The net force is still zero, but $\\tau = pE\\sin 90^\\circ = pE$, the maximum value. So B-2.\n(C) The torque is zero (parallel), but the non-uniform field gives a net force towards increasing field. So C-3.\n(D) The torque is zero (antiparallel), and the net force points towards decreasing field. So D-4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a glass rod is rubbed with silk, the rod becomes positively charged because some electrons move from the rod to the silk.\nStatement II: Rubbing creates new positive charges on the glass rod and an equal amount of new negative charge on the silk.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. Loosely bound electrons move from the glass to the silk, so the glass is left with a deficit of electrons (positive) and the silk has an excess (negative).\nStatement II: Incorrect. No new charge is created by rubbing. Charge is only transferred from one body to the other, so the total charge is conserved.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A proton is placed in a uniform electric field of magnitude $5.0 \\times 10^{3}$ N/C. What is the magnitude of its acceleration? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg; ignore gravity.)","image":"","options":["$4.79 \\times 10^{11}$ m/s$^2$","$8.78 \\times 10^{14}$ m/s$^2$","$2.40 \\times 10^{11}$ m/s$^2$","$9.58 \\times 10^{11}$ m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E = 5.0 \\times 10^{3}$ N/C, $q = e$, $m = m_p$.\nFormula: $a = \\frac{eE}{m_p}$.\nSubstituting: $a = \\frac{1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 5.0 \\times 10^{3}}{1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}$.\nSo $a \\approx 4.79 \\times 10^{11}$ m/s$^2$.\nOption (2) is the value you get by using the electron mass instead.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electric dipole of dipole moment $4 \\times 10^{-9}$ C m is placed in a uniform electric field of $5 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C. Its dipole moment makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the field. What is the magnitude of the torque on the dipole?","image":"","options":["$1 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m","$1.73 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m","$2 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m","$5 \\times 10^{-5}$ N m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p = 4 \\times 10^{-9}$ C m, $E = 5 \\times 10^4$ N/C, $\\theta = 30^\\circ$.\nFormula: $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}$, so $\\tau = pE\\sin\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $\\tau = 4 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 5 \\times 10^4 \\times 0.5$.\nSo $\\tau = 1 \\times 10^{-4}$ N m.\nOption (2) uses $\\cos 30^\\circ$ instead of $\\sin 30^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a region, the electric field has components $E_x = \\alpha x^{1/2}$ and $E_y = E_z = 0$, where $\\alpha = 600\\ N\\,C^{-1}m^{-1/2}$. A cube of side $a = 0.2$ m has its faces parallel to the coordinate planes. Its left face is at $x = a$ and its right face is at $x = 2a$. What is the charge enclosed by the cube? Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854 \\times 10^{-12}\\ C^2N^{-1}m^{-2}$.","image":"","options":["39.4 pC","134.4 pC","229.4 pC","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the faces perpendicular to x carry flux. The other four faces have $\\vec{E}$ parallel to them.\nLeft face ($x = a$): the outward normal is along $-\\hat{i}$, so $\\phi_L = -\\alpha a^{1/2}a^2$.\nRight face ($x = 2a$): $\\phi_R = +\\alpha(2a)^{1/2}a^2$.\nNet flux: $\\phi = \\alpha a^{5/2}(\\sqrt{2} - 1) = 600 \\times 0.01789 \\times 0.4142 = 4.45\\ N\\,m^2C^{-1}$.\nCharge: $q = \\varepsilon_0\\phi = 3.94 \\times 10^{-11}\\ C = 39.4$ pC.\nIgnoring the sign of the left face gives 229.4 pC. Taking only the right face gives 134.4 pC.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about quantisation of electric charge is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The charge on any body can be written as $q = ne$, where n is a positive or negative integer.","Quantisation of charge was experimentally demonstrated by Millikan in 1912.","Quantisation was first suggested by Coulomb's torsion balance measurements of forces between charged spheres.","For macroscopic charges of the order of mC, quantisation has no practical consequence."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: every free charge is an integral multiple of e.\n(2) Correct: Millikan demonstrated quantisation experimentally in 1912.\n(3) Incorrect: quantisation was first suggested by Faraday's laws of electrolysis. Coulomb's torsion balance gave the inverse-square force law, not the idea of quantisation.\n(4) Correct: 1 mC is about $10^{13}$ times e, so the steps of size e are not noticeable and charge appears continuous.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a region the electric field has components $E_x = \\alpha x^{1/2}$ and $E_y = E_z = 0$, with $\\alpha = 600$ N C$^{-1}$ m$^{-1/2}$. A cube of side a = 0.2 m has its faces perpendicular to the x-axis at x = a and x = 2a, and its other faces parallel to the x-axis. What is the charge enclosed by the cube? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$3.94\\times10^{-11}$ C","$9.50\\times10^{-11}$ C","$1.97\\times10^{-11}$ C","$13.44\\times10^{-11}$ C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the faces perpendicular to x have non-zero flux.\nLeft face (x = a): the outward normal is along $-x$, so $\\phi_L = -\\alpha a^{1/2}a^2$.\nRight face (x = 2a): $\\phi_R = +\\alpha(2a)^{1/2}a^2$.\nNet flux: $\\phi = \\alpha a^{5/2}(\\sqrt{2}-1) = 600\\times0.01789\\times0.4142 \\approx 4.45$ N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$.\nBy Gauss's law, $q = \\varepsilon_0\\phi \\approx 3.94\\times10^{-11}$ C.\nThe value $13.44\\times10^{-11}$ C uses only the right face.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (charge configuration) with List-II (field line pattern).\nList-I:\n(A) Isolated charge +q\n(B) Isolated charge –q\n(C) Two equal positive charges (q, q)\n(D) Dipole (q, –q)\nList-II:\n(I) Lines come radially from infinity and end on the charge\n(II) Lines leave one charge and end on the other, showing attraction\n(III) Lines go radially outward to infinity\n(IV) Lines from both charges bend away from each other, showing repulsion","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The field of a positive charge is radially outward, so A matches III.\n(B) The field of a negative charge is radially inward, ending on the charge, so B matches I.\n(C) The lines of (q, q) push away from each other, picturing repulsion, so C matches IV.\n(D) The lines of a dipole run from +q to –q, picturing attraction, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ratio of the magnitude of the electrostatic force to that of the gravitational force between two protons is the same at every separation.\nReason (R): Both the Coulomb force and the Newtonian gravitational force between point objects vary as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $F_e = \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$ and $F_G = \\frac{Gm_p^2}{r^2}$. Both are inverse-square laws.\nA is true: in the ratio $\\frac{F_e}{F_G} = \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 G m_p^2}$, the $r^2$ cancels. The ratio is about $1.3 \\times 10^{36}$ at every separation.\nThe cancellation happens only because of the common $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ dependence. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An electric dipole placed with its dipole moment antiparallel to a non-uniform electric field experiences a net force in the direction of decreasing field, but no torque.\nStatement II: A permanent dipole in a uniform electric field experiences zero net force, though it may experience a torque.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When $\\vec{p}$ is antiparallel to $\\vec{E}$, the torque $\\vec{p}\\times\\vec{E}$ is zero. In a non-uniform field, the net force then points toward decreasing field, as NCERT's Fig. 1.20 shows.\nStatement II is correct. In a uniform field the forces qE and $-qE$ cancel. They act at different points, however, so a couple $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p}\\times\\vec{E}$ acts unless $\\vec{p}$ is parallel or antiparallel to $\\vec{E}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An insulated metal sphere A initially carries a charge of $-8\\ \\mu C$. Electrons are removed from it at a steady rate of $5 \\times 10^{12}$ electrons per second for 20 s. Sphere A is then touched to an identical, uncharged, insulated metal sphere B and separated. What is the final charge on B? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C)","image":"","options":["$+4\\ \\mu C$","$+8\\ \\mu C$","$-4\\ \\mu C$","$+12\\ \\mu C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: initial charge $q_0 = -8\\ \\mu C$, rate $= 5 \\times 10^{12}$ electrons per second, time $t = 20$ s.\nElectrons removed: $n = 5 \\times 10^{12} \\times 20 = 10^{14}$. Removing electrons adds positive charge $ne = 10^{14} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} = 1.6 \\times 10^{-5}$ C $= +16\\ \\mu C$.\nBy additivity, the charge on A is $-8 + 16 = +8\\ \\mu C$.\nOn contact, total charge is conserved and shared equally by symmetry. So B gets $\\frac{8}{2} = +4\\ \\mu C$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+1\\ \\mu C$, $+1\\ \\mu C$ and $-1\\ \\mu C$ are placed at the vertices A, B and C of an equilateral triangle of side 0.3 m in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the net electrostatic force on the $-1\\ \\mu C$ charge at C? ($k = 9 \\times 10^9\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.173 N","0.2 N","0.1 N","0.141 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: each pair force is $F = \\frac{kq^2}{l^2} = \\frac{9 \\times 10^9 \\times (10^{-6})^2}{0.09} = 0.1$ N.\nThe charge at C is pulled towards A and towards B. These two equal forces are $60^\\circ$ apart.\nFormula: $F_{net} = 2F\\cos 30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F$, directed along the bisector of angle ACB.\nSo $F_{net} = 1.732 \\times 0.1 = 0.173$ N.\nOption (2) adds the forces as if they were parallel. Option (4) treats them as perpendicular.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical small conducting spheres carry charges $+8\\ \\mu C$ and $-2\\ \\mu C$ and are held 30 cm apart in vacuum. They are made to touch each other and are then returned to the same separation. What is the magnitude of the force between them after contact? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["1.6 N","0.9 N","0.4 N","2.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $q_1 = +8\\ \\mu C$, $q_2 = -2\\ \\mu C$, r = 0.30 m. The initial attractive force is $\\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 16 \\times 10^{-12}}{0.09} = 1.6$ N.\nOn contact, the total charge $+6\\ \\mu C$ is conserved (additivity). It divides equally between the identical spheres, giving $+3\\ \\mu C$ each.\nSubstituting: $F' = \\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 9 \\times 10^{-12}}{0.09}$\nSo F' = 0.9 N, and the force is now repulsive.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the magnitude of each of two point charges is halved and the separation between them is also halved, the electrostatic force between them becomes half of its original value.\nStatement II: Coulomb obtained known fractions $\\frac{q}{2}$, $\\frac{q}{4}$, ... of an unknown charge $q$ by repeatedly touching the charged sphere with an identical uncharged sphere.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The new force is $F' = k\\frac{(q/2)(q'/2)}{(r/2)^2} = k\\frac{qq'}{r^2} = F$, so the force is unchanged, not halved.\nStatement II is correct. By symmetry, the charge spreads equally over two identical spheres. This let Coulomb vary charges in known ratios without knowing their absolute values.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The number of field lines of a point charge crossing a sphere centred on the charge is the same, whatever the radius of the sphere.\nReason (R): The field of a point charge falls as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, while the area of the enclosing sphere grows as $r^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: no lines begin or end between two concentric spheres, so every line crossing the inner sphere also crosses the outer one.\nR is true: $E \\propto \\frac{1}{r^2}$, and the area is $4\\pi r^2$.\nThe number of lines crossing is proportional to $E \\times$ area, which is independent of r. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a drawing, 3140 field lines leave an isolated positive point charge uniformly in all directions. A small flat area of $2\\ \\text{cm}^2$ is placed 5 cm from the charge. Its normal makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the radial direction. Take $\\pi = 3.14$ and treat the field as uniform over the small area. About how many field lines cross the area?","image":"","options":["5","10","17","20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: N = 3140, $\\Delta S = 2\\ \\text{cm}^2$, r = 5 cm, $\\theta = 60^\\circ$.\nLine density at r: $\\frac{N}{4\\pi r^2} = \\frac{3140}{314}$ = 10 lines per $\\text{cm}^2$.\nOnly the projected area matters: $\\Delta S \\cos\\theta = 2 \\times 0.5 = 1\\ \\text{cm}^2$.\nSo the lines crossing = 10 × 1 = 10.\nIgnoring the tilt would give the wrong answer, 20.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Bringing a third charge $q_3$ near $q_1$ changes the force exerted on $q_1$ by $q_2$.\n(ii) The total force on a charge due to several charges is found by adding the individual forces using the parallelogram law.\n(iii) Coulomb's law by itself is sufficient to find the net force on a charge in a system of many charges.\n(iv) All of electrostatics is basically a consequence of Coulomb's law and the superposition principle.\n(v) The electric field due to a system of charges also obeys the superposition principle.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: individual pair forces are unaffected by the other charges.\n(ii) Correct: forces are added as vectors.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT notes that Coulomb's law alone is not enough; superposition is also needed.\n(iv) Correct: this is stated directly in NCERT.\n(v) Correct: $\\vec{E} = \\vec{E}_1 + \\vec{E}_2 + \\ldots$\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In the early atomic model of NCERT Example 1.12, a point nucleus of charge $+Ze$ is surrounded by a uniform negative charge cloud of radius $R$, and the atom is neutral overall. Take $Z = 1$ and $R = 1.0\\times10^{-10}$ m. Find the magnitude of the electric field at $r = 0.5\\times10^{-10}$ m from the nucleus. ($e = 1.6\\times10^{-19}$ C, $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N $m^2$ $C^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$5.04\\times10^{11}$ N/C","$5.76\\times10^{11}$ N/C","$7.2\\times10^{10}$ N/C","$6.48\\times10^{11}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Z = 1$, $R = 10^{-10}$ m and $r = R/2$, so the point lies inside the cloud.\nFormula: by Gauss's law, $E = kZe\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2} - \\frac{r}{R^3}\\right)$ for $r < R$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{r^2} = 4\\times10^{20}$ and $\\frac{r}{R^3} = 0.5\\times10^{20}$. Also $kZe = 1.44\\times10^{-9}$.\nSo $E = 1.44\\times10^{-9}\\times3.5\\times10^{20} = 5.04\\times10^{11}$ N/C, directed radially outward.\nThe value $5.76\\times10^{11}$ N/C ignores the enclosed negative charge.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"After rubbing with silk, a glass rod carries a charge of $+4.8 \\times 10^{-9}$ C. How many electrons were transferred from the rod to the silk? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.)","image":"","options":["$1.5 \\times 10^{10}$","$3.0 \\times 10^{10}$","$3.0 \\times 10^{9}$","$6.0 \\times 10^{10}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: q = $4.8 \\times 10^{-9}$ C, e = $1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nFormula: The rod becomes positive by losing electrons, so q = ne and n = q/e.\nSubstituting: n = $\\frac{4.8 \\times 10^{-9}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$.\nSo n = $3.0 \\times 10^{10}$ electrons, all transferred from the rod to the silk.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges, each of $+1$ C, are placed 1 m apart in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the electrostatic force between them? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ N","$9 \\times 10^{9}$ N","1 N","$9 \\times 10^{-9}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $q_1 = q_2 = 1$ C, $r = 1$ m, $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{kq_1q_2}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $F = 9 \\times 10^{9} \\times \\frac{1 \\times 1}{1^2}$\nSo $F = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N. This is how the coulomb is defined. The huge value shows that 1 C is a very large unit.\nThe value $8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ is $\\varepsilon_0$, which is not a force.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In ordinary laboratory electrostatics, the charge on a body can be treated as varying continuously.\nReason (R): A charge of about $1\\ \\mu C$ contains roughly $10^{13}$ electronic charges, so a change of one unit $e$ is far too small to notice.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: at the macroscopic level, quantisation has no practical consequence, and charge appears continuous.\nR is true: $\\frac{10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} \\approx 6 \\times 10^{12}$, which is of the order of $10^{13}$.\nR explains A: because the step size $e$ is tiny compared with the total charge, the grainy nature is lost. This is like a dotted line that looks continuous from a distance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The charge on any body can be increased or decreased only in steps of $e$.\nStatement II: A body containing $n_1$ electrons and $n_2$ protons carries a net charge of $(n_1 - n_2)e$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the net charge is $(n_2 - n_1)e$, and $n_2 - n_1$ is an integer. So charge can change only in steps of $e$.\nStatement II is incorrect: the net charge is $n_2(+e) + n_1(-e) = (n_2 - n_1)e$, not $(n_1 - n_2)e$. The given expression has the wrong sign.\nFor example, a body with excess protons is positive, but the given formula would make it negative.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform electric field $E$ points along $+x$. Match List-I with List-II (ignore gravity; particles start from rest).\nList-I\nA. Direction of force on an electron\nB. Direction of force on a proton\nC. Magnitude of acceleration of the proton\nD. Time taken by the proton to move a distance $h$\nList-II\n(i) $\\sqrt{\\frac{2hm_p}{eE}}$\n(ii) along $+x$\n(iii) along $-x$\n(iv) $\\frac{eE}{m_p}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The force on a negative charge is opposite to $E$, so it acts along $-x$ → (iii).\nB: The force on a positive charge is along $E$, so it acts along $+x$ → (ii).\nC: $a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{eE}{m_p}$ → (iv).\nD: From $h = \\frac{1}{2}at^2$, $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{a}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm_p}{eE}}$ → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (charge configuration) with List-II (dependence of field magnitude on distance $r$):\nList-I\n(A) Point charge\n(B) Infinitely long uniformly charged straight wire\n(C) Infinite uniformly charged plane sheet\n(D) Electric dipole, at a point far away ($r \\gg a$)\nList-II\n(I) Independent of $r$\n(II) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r^3}$\n(III) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r}$\n(IV) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r^2}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) $E = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$, so A matches (IV).\n(B) $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$, so B matches (III).\n(C) $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$, which is independent of distance, so C matches (I).\n(D) $E = \\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ on the axis and $\\frac{p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ on the equatorial plane, so D matches (II).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about frictional electricity:\n(i) Two plastic rods rubbed with cat's fur repel each other, and each rod also repels the fur.\n(ii) A glass rod rubbed with silk repels the cat's fur that was used to rub a plastic rod.\n(iii) A plastic rod rubbed with fur repels the silk that was used to rub a glass rod.\n(iv) In a gold-leaf electroscope, the degree of divergence of the leaves indicates the amount of charge.\n(v) When a body is charged by rubbing, the electrons transferred form a large fraction of all the electrons in the body.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the plastic rod and the fur carry unlike charges, so the rod attracts the fur.\n(ii) Correct: the glass rod and the fur are both positive by convention, so they repel.\n(iii) Correct: the plastic rod and the silk are both negative, so they repel.\n(iv) Correct: greater divergence of the leaves means more charge.\n(v) Incorrect: only a very small fraction of the electrons is transferred.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"The ratio of the magnitude of the electric force to the gravitational force between an electron and a proton is approximately","image":"","options":["$1.3 \\times 10^{36}$, and it decreases as the separation increases","$2.4 \\times 10^{39}$, independent of the separation","$2.4 \\times 10^{39}$, and it increases as the separation increases","$1.3 \\times 10^{36}$, independent of the separation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For an electron-proton pair, $\\frac{F_e}{F_G} = \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 G m_p m_e} \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{39}$.\nBoth forces vary as $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, so r cancels and the ratio does not depend on the separation.\n$1.3 \\times 10^{36}$ is the ratio for two protons. It is smaller because $m_p m_p > m_p m_e$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electric field at a point due to a source charge Q is defined as $\\vec{E} = \\lim_{q \\to 0} \\frac{\\vec{F}}{q}$, where q is the test charge.\nReason (R): A test charge of finite size would exert a force on the source charge Q, which could move Q from its original location.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT defines the field as the limit of F/q as the test charge tends to zero.\nR is true. By Newton's third law, q exerts an equal force on Q, and the source charge would tend to move.\nTaking q negligibly small makes this disturbing force negligible. The ratio F/q still stays finite.\nSo R is exactly why the limit is taken, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A flat surface of area $0.05\\ m^2$ is placed in a uniform electric field of magnitude $400\\ N\\,C^{-1}$. The normal to the surface makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the field. What is the electric flux through the surface?","image":"","options":["$10\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","$17.3\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","$20\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E = 400\\ N\\,C^{-1}$, $\\Delta S = 0.05\\ m^2$, and $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ between $\\vec{E}$ and the area vector (the normal).\nFormula: $\\Delta\\phi = \\vec{E}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{S} = E\\,\\Delta S\\cos\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta\\phi = 400 \\times 0.05 \\times \\cos 60^\\circ = 20 \\times 0.5$.\nSo $\\Delta\\phi = 10\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$.\nUsing $\\sin\\theta$ wrongly gives 17.3. Zero would need the normal to be perpendicular to $\\vec{E}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the Gauss's law result $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$ for a uniformly charged straight wire is correct?","image":"","options":["For a wire of finite length, the result holds approximately near its central portion, where end effects can be ignored","The flat end faces of the cylindrical Gaussian surface contribute the major part of the total flux","The field at the Gaussian surface is produced only by the charge $\\lambda l$ enclosed by the cylinder","The result holds exactly for a short wire, as long as the Gaussian cylinder is coaxial with it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Option 1: Correct. NCERT states the formula is approximately true around the central portions of a long wire, where end effects may be ignored.\nOption 2: Wrong. The field is radial, so it is parallel to the flat end faces and their flux is zero.\nOption 3: Wrong. Only $\\lambda l$ appears in Gauss's law, but the field itself is due to the charge on the entire wire.\nOption 4: Wrong. The derivation needs an infinite wire, so that $E$ is normal to the curved surface everywhere.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $q$, $q$ and $-q$ are held at the vertices of an equilateral triangle.\nAssertion (A): The vector sum of the net forces acting on the three charges is zero.\nReason (R): Coulomb's law is consistent with Newton's third law, i.e. $\\vec{F}_{12} = -\\vec{F}_{21}$ for every pair.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT Example 1.6 shows that $\\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 + \\vec{F}_3 = 0$.\nR is true: for any pair, $\\vec{F}_{12} = -\\vec{F}_{21}$.\nWhen all net forces are added, every pair force cancels with its reaction. That is exactly why the total is zero, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A small metal sphere is given a charge of $+3.2\\ \\mu C$. How many electrons must have been removed from it? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C)","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{12}$","$2 \\times 10^{13}$","$5 \\times 10^{13}$","$2 \\times 10^{14}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $q = +3.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ C and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. The sphere becomes positive by losing electrons.\nFormula: $q = ne$, so $n = \\frac{q}{e}$.\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{3.2 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = 2 \\times 10^{13}$.\nSo $2 \\times 10^{13}$ electrons were removed. Option (1) results from slipping one power of ten.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A large flat non-conducting sheet carries a uniform surface charge density $\\sigma = 1.77\\times10^{-7}$ C/m$^2$. What is the magnitude of the electric field near its middle region? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.0\\times10^{4}$ N/C","$2.0\\times10^{4}$ N/C","$5.0\\times10^{3}$ N/C","$1.0\\times10^{2}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\sigma = 1.77\\times10^{-7}$ C/m$^2$. Near the middle of a large sheet, end effects are negligible, so the sheet can be treated as infinite.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{1.77\\times10^{-7}}{2\\times8.85\\times10^{-12}}$.\nSo $E = 1.0\\times10^4$ N/C.\nThe option $2.0\\times10^4$ N/C comes from wrongly using $\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The macroscopic surface charge density $\\sigma$ is a smoothed-out average that ignores the quantisation of charge.\n(ii) The element $\\Delta V$ used to define $\\rho$ is small on the macroscopic scale but contains a very large number of microscopic charged constituents.\n(iii) A Gaussian surface may pass through a continuous charge distribution but should not pass through a discrete point charge.\n(iv) In the Gauss's law derivation for an infinite line charge, the field on the Gaussian surface is produced only by the enclosed charge $\\lambda l$.\n(v) The field of a uniformly charged infinite plane sheet decreases inversely with distance from the sheet.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\sigma$ is a macroscopic average that ignores the discreteness of charge.\n(ii) Correct: this is how NCERT chooses the elements $\\Delta l$, $\\Delta S$ and $\\Delta V$.\n(iii) Correct: the field is undefined at a point charge, but finite within a continuous distribution.\n(iv) Incorrect: only $\\lambda l$ appears in Gauss's law, but E itself is due to the whole wire.\n(v) Incorrect: the sheet's field $\\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$ is independent of distance.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A point charge of $+2\\,\\mu C$ is placed in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the electric field it produces at a point 30 cm away? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} = 9\\times10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$2.0\\times10^{5}$ N/C","$6.0\\times10^{4}$ N/C","$2.0\\times10^{6}$ N/C","$4.0\\times10^{5}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Q = 2\\times10^{-6}$ C and $r = 0.30$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}\\frac{Q}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^{9}\\times2\\times10^{-6}}{(0.30)^2} = \\frac{1.8\\times10^{4}}{0.09}$\nSo E = $2.0\\times10^{5}$ N/C.\nThe option $6.0\\times10^{4}$ N/C comes from forgetting to square r.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are classified as conductors in NCERT?\nCopper wire, human body, earth, glass, porcelain, nylon, wood","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Copper wire (a metal): conductor.\nHuman body: conductor.\nEarth: conductor.\nGlass, porcelain, nylon and wood: insulators, because they offer high resistance to the passage of electricity.\nSo 3 of the listed items are conductors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about electric field lines are correct?\n(i) In a region of uniform electric field, the field lines are parallel, equally spaced straight lines.\n(ii) For an isolated point charge, the number of field lines crossing a concentric sphere decreases as the radius of the sphere increases.\n(iii) The solid angle subtended at a point by a small plane area $\\Delta S$, placed perpendicular to the line joining it to the point at distance $r$, is $\\Delta \\Omega = \\frac{\\Delta S}{r^2}$.\n(iv) In a charge-free region, electrostatic field lines may form closed loops.\n(v) The field line pattern of two equal positive charges $(q, q)$ pictures their mutual repulsion.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a constant field is shown by uniformly spaced parallel straight lines.\n(ii) Incorrect: E falls as $1/r^2$ while the sphere's area grows as $r^2$, so the number of lines crossing it stays constant.\n(iii) Correct: this is the definition of solid angle, $\\Delta\\Omega = \\Delta S/r^2$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the electrostatic field is conservative, so its field lines never form closed loops.\n(v) Correct: the lines from the two charges bend away from each other, showing repulsion.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity) with List-II (SI unit).\nList-I:\n(A) Electric flux\n(B) Linear charge density\n(C) Permittivity of free space $\\varepsilon_0$\n(D) Electric field\nList-II:\n(i) C/m\n(ii) N/C\n(iii) N m$^2$/C\n(iv) C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Flux is $\\vec{E}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{S}$, so its unit is (N/C)(m$^2$) = N m$^2$/C, which is (iii).\n(B) $\\lambda = \\Delta Q/\\Delta l$, so its unit is C/m, which is (i).\n(C) $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$, which is (iv).\n(D) From $F = qE$, the unit of E is N/C, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electric field line pictures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For charges +2q and –q, only half of the lines leaving +2q end on –q; the rest go to infinity.","In a plane drawing of a point charge's field, the number of lines per unit length of a circle around the charge correctly shows the $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ fall of the field.","Electrostatic field lines cannot form closed loops because the electrostatic field is conservative.","No field line passes through the point midway between two equal positive charges."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the number of lines is proportional to charge, so –q receives only half of the lines from +2q.\n(2) Incorrect: in 2D, the lines through a circle of length $2\\pi r$ give a density falling as $\\frac{1}{r}$. The $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ fall appears only for lines per unit area in 3D, as NCERT stresses.\n(3) Correct, as stated in the chapter.\n(4) Correct: E = 0 at the midpoint, so no direction exists there.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Coulomb's experimental work are correct?\n(i) He measured the force between charged metallic spheres using a torsion balance.\n(ii) He knew the exact magnitudes of the charges on the spheres before starting his measurements.\n(iii) Touching a charged metal sphere with an identical uncharged sphere leaves half the original charge on each sphere.\n(iv) Coulomb's law has since been verified down to distances of about $10^{-10}$ m.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Coulomb used a torsion balance to measure the force.\n(ii) Incorrect: the charges were unknown at the start. He compared forces using fractions such as q/2 and q/4.\n(iii) Correct: by symmetry, the charge shares equally between identical spheres, giving q/2 on each.\n(iv) Correct: the law holds down to the subatomic level, $r \\sim 10^{-10}$ m.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A closed Gaussian surface encloses point charges $+2\\ \\mu C$, $-5\\ \\mu C$ and $+1\\ \\mu C$. A charge of $+4\\ \\mu C$ lies just outside the surface. What is the net electric flux through the surface? ($\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854 \\times 10^{-12}\\ C^2 N^{-1} m^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$-2.26 \\times 10^{5}\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","$+2.26 \\times 10^{5}\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","$+9.04 \\times 10^{5}\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$","$-5.65 \\times 10^{5}\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the enclosed charge is $q = (2 - 5 + 1)\\ \\mu C = -2\\ \\mu C$. The outside charge does not count.\nFormula: $\\phi = q/\\varepsilon_0$ (Gauss's law).\nSubstituting: $\\phi = \\frac{-2 \\times 10^{-6}}{8.854 \\times 10^{-12}} = -2.26 \\times 10^5\\ N\\,m^2\\,C^{-1}$.\nThe outside charge does change the field on the surface, but it does not change the net flux. Including it would wrongly give the positive value in option (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The electric field of an isolated negative point charge points radially inwards, towards the charge, at every point around it.\nStatement II: The magnitude of the electric field of a point charge is the same at all points on a sphere centred on the charge.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The field of a positive source charge points radially outward. The field of a negative source charge points radially inward.\nStatement II is correct. $E = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{|Q|}{r^2}$ depends only on the distance r.\nSo E has the same magnitude everywhere on a sphere centred on Q. This is called spherical symmetry.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A neutral metal sphere on an insulating stand acquires a charge of $+3.2\\ \\mu C$. Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. How many electrons were removed from it?","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{13}$","$2 \\times 10^{12}$","$5.12 \\times 10^{-25}$","$2 \\times 10^{25}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 3.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ C and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nFormula: $q = ne$, so $n = \\frac{q}{e}$.\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{3.2 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = 2 \\times 10^{13}$.\nThe body becomes positive because it loses electrons.\nOption (3) comes from multiplying instead of dividing, and n must be a large whole number.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Linear charge density $\\lambda$\n(B) Surface charge density $\\sigma$\n(C) Volume charge density $\\rho$\n(D) Electric flux $\\phi$\nList-II:\n(i) C m$^{-3}$\n(ii) N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$\n(iii) C m$^{-1}$\n(iv) C m$^{-2}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\lambda = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta l}$, so its unit is C/m, which is (iii).\n(B) $\\sigma = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta S}$, so its unit is C/m$^2$, which is (iv).\n(C) $\\rho = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta V}$, so its unit is C/m$^3$, which is (i).\n(D) Flux is E times area, so its unit is (N/C)(m$^2$) = N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two concentric thin spherical shells carry uniformly spread charges. The inner shell has radius 5 cm and charge +2 nC. The outer shell has radius 15 cm and charge −6 nC. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 10 cm from the common centre? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N $m^2$ $C^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["1800 N/C","3600 N/C","7200 N/C","0 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the point is at $r = 0.1$ m. This is outside the inner shell but inside the outer shell.\nThe outer shell produces zero field at all points inside it. Equivalently, a Gaussian sphere of radius 10 cm encloses only the +2 nC.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{kq_{inner}}{r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times2\\times10^{-9}}{(0.1)^2} = 1800$ N/C, directed outward.\nThe value 3600 N/C wrongly uses the net charge of −4 nC.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Electric and magnetic fields are regarded as physical entities and not merely as mathematical constructs.\nReason (R): Fields have an independent dynamics of their own and can transport energy, as seen when a source of time-dependent fields is switched on briefly and then switched off.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that fields are physical entities even though they are detected only through forces on charges.\nR is true. Fields evolve according to their own laws. A source switched on briefly and then off leaves behind propagating electromagnetic fields that carry energy.\nThis independent existence and energy transport is exactly why fields are treated as real. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Why can two electrostatic field lines never cross each other at a point?","image":"","options":["At the point of intersection, the electric field would have two different directions, which is impossible","Electrostatic field lines always form closed loops, and closed loops cannot intersect","The field becomes infinitely strong wherever two lines meet","Field lines from like charges always repel each other and so bend away"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The tangent to a field line at any point gives the direction of the net electric field there.\nIf two lines crossed, there would be two tangents at that point, so the field would have two directions. This is absurd, because the net field at a point is unique.\nOption (2) is false because electrostatic field lines never form closed loops.\nOptions (3) and (4) are not reasons given in NCERT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A charge $Q$ placed at the centroid of an equilateral triangle, with equal charges $q$ at the three vertices, experiences zero net electrostatic force.\nReason (R): The three forces on $Q$ have equal magnitudes and are directed at $120^\\circ$ to one another, so their vector sum vanishes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The centroid is equidistant from all three vertices, at distance $\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{3}}$. So each force has magnitude $\\frac{3Qq}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$.\nR is true. The forces act along AO, BO and CO, which are mutually at $120^\\circ$.\nThe resultant of the forces from B and C is equal in magnitude to the force from A but points along OA, opposite to it. So the total is zero.\nThe symmetry in R is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron and a proton are each released from rest in the same uniform electric field and fall through the same distance. Gravity is neglected, and the field is reversed for the proton so that both move in the same direction.\nStatement I: The proton takes roughly 43 times as long as the electron to cover the distance.\nStatement II: The time of fall is proportional to $\\sqrt{m}$, because the acceleration $\\frac{eE}{m}$ is inversely proportional to the mass of the particle. ($m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg, $m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg)","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: the force $eE$ is the same for both particles, so $a = \\frac{eE}{m}$. From $h = \\frac{1}{2}at^2$, $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm}{eE}} \\propto \\sqrt{m}$. True.\nStatement I: $\\frac{t_p}{t_e} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}}} \\approx \\sqrt{1833} \\approx 43$. True.\nThis is unlike free fall under gravity, where the time of fall does not depend on mass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth. Which of the following correctly describes what happens?","image":"","options":["Protons move from the silk to the glass rod, so the rod becomes positively charged","Electrons move from the glass rod to the silk, so the rod becomes positively charged and the silk negatively charged","Electrons move from the silk to the glass rod, so the rod becomes negatively charged","Positive and negative charges are newly created in equal amounts on the rod and the silk"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In solids, only the loosely bound electrons are transferred from one body to another.\nDuring rubbing, some electrons leave the glass rod and go to the silk. The rod is left with a deficit of electrons, so it becomes positive. The silk gains electrons, so it becomes negative.\nOption (1) is wrong because protons are not transferred.\nOption (4) is wrong because rubbing does not create any new charge; it only transfers existing charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Faraday\n(B) Millikan\n(C) Benjamin Franklin\n(D) Coulomb\nList-II\n(i) Named the two kinds of charge positive and negative\n(ii) His laws of electrolysis first suggested that charge is quantised\n(iii) Arrived at the inverse square law of electric force using a torsion balance\n(iv) Experimentally demonstrated quantisation of charge in 1912","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Faraday's experimental laws of electrolysis first suggested that charge comes in discrete units.\nB-(iv): Millikan experimentally demonstrated quantisation of charge in 1912.\nC-(i): Benjamin Franklin named the two kinds of charge positive and negative.\nD-(iii): Coulomb used a torsion balance and arrived at the inverse square law in 1785.\nOption (2) swaps Faraday and Millikan, so it is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A point charge of $2 \\times 10^{-9}$ C is placed at the centre of a spherical surface of radius 10 cm. What is the total electric flux through the sphere? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["226 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","113 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","452 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","18 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: q = $2\\times10^{-9}$ C, enclosed by the sphere.\nFormula: $\\phi = q/\\varepsilon_0$. The radius does not matter, because E falls as $1/r^2$ while the area grows as $r^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\phi = 2\\times10^{-9}/8.854\\times10^{-12}$.\nSo $\\phi \\approx$ 226 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$. The value 18 is $kq$, which is not the flux.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A metal spoon held in the bare hand does not show signs of charging when rubbed with wool.\nReason (R): Both the metal and the human body are conductors, so the charge leaks through the body to the ground.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT contrasts a plastic comb, which gets charged on rubbing, with a metal spoon, which does not.\nR is true: metals, the human body and the earth are all conductors.\nAny charge produced on the spoon therefore flows through the hand and body to the earth. That is exactly why the spoon shows no charge, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+2\\ \\mu C$, $+2\\ \\mu C$ and $-2\\ \\mu C$ are placed at the vertices A, B and C respectively of an equilateral triangle of side 0.3 m in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the net electrostatic force on the charge at C? (Take $k = 9\\times10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.40 N","0.69 N","0.80 N","0.35 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: |q| = $2\\times10^{-6}$ C and l = 0.3 m.\nEach pair force: $F = \\frac{kq^2}{l^2} = \\frac{9\\times10^9\\times4\\times10^{-12}}{0.09} = 0.4$ N.\nAt C, both forces are attractions, one along CA and one along CB, with 60° between them.\nResultant: $2F\\cos30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F = 1.732\\times0.4$, directed along the bisector of angle BCA.\nSo F = 0.69 N. Option (3), 0.80 N, wrongly adds the two forces as if they were parallel.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (net outward electric flux).\nList-I:\n(A) Closed sphere enclosing charges +2q and $-q$\n(B) Closed cube enclosing no charge, with a charge q placed just outside it\n(C) Closed cylinder enclosing a single charge $-q$\n(D) One face of a cube that has charge q at its centre\nList-II:\n(I) $\\frac{q}{6\\varepsilon_0}$\n(II) $-\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}$\n(III) Zero\n(IV) $\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Net enclosed charge = 2q $-$ q = q, so flux = $q/\\varepsilon_0$, which is IV.\n(B) No enclosed charge gives zero net flux, even though the field from the outside charge is non-zero on the cube. This is III.\n(C) Enclosed charge $-q$ gives flux $-q/\\varepsilon_0$, which is II.\n(D) The total flux $q/\\varepsilon_0$ is shared equally by 6 symmetric faces, giving $q/6\\varepsilon_0$. This is I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+q$ at A, $+q$ at B and $-q$ at C sit at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side $l$. Let $F = \\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 l^2}$.\nStatement I: The net force on the charge at A has magnitude $F$ and is directed parallel to BC.\nStatement II: The net force on the charge at C has magnitude $2F$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. At A there is a repulsion $F$ along BA and an attraction $F$ along AC. These two vectors make $120^\\circ$ with each other, so the resultant is $F$, along BC.\nStatement II is incorrect. At C the attractions towards A and towards B are $F$ each, with $60^\\circ$ between them. The resultant is $2F\\cos 30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F$, along the bisector of angle BCA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges $+2$ nC and $-2$ nC are separated by 2 mm. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point on the equatorial line 10 cm from the centre of the dipole? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["3.6 N/C","18 N/C","36 N/C","72 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: q = $2 \\times 10^{-9}$ C, 2a = $2 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, r = 0.1 m. Since r >> a, use the far-field formula.\nDipole moment: $p = q \\times 2a = 4 \\times 10^{-12}$ C m.\nFormula (equatorial): $E = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}\\frac{p}{r^3}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^9 \\times 4\\times10^{-12}}{10^{-3}} = 36$ N/C.\n72 N/C is the axial value at the same distance, which carries an extra factor of 2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A metal rod fitted with a plastic handle is held by the handle and rubbed with wool, without the metal part touching the hand. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The rod becomes charged because the insulating handle prevents charge from leaking through the body to the ground","The charge acquired by the metal part spreads over its entire surface","If the metal part is later touched with a bare hand, the rod loses its charge to the earth","The charge stays confined to the spot that was rubbed, just as it would on a plastic rod"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the plastic handle blocks the conducting path through the body to the earth, so the rod shows signs of charging.\n(2) Correct: charge given to a conductor readily spreads over its entire surface.\n(3) Correct: touching the metal provides a conducting path, and the charge leaks through the body to the ground.\n(4) Incorrect: charge stays in place only on an insulator. On a metal it spreads out.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field at a point on the axis of a short dipole, at a distance r from its centre ($r \\gg a$), is 160 N/C. What is the field at a point on the same axis at distance 2r from the centre?","image":"","options":["80 N/C","40 N/C","20 N/C","10 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $E = 160$ N/C at distance r on the axis.\nFormula: far from a dipole, $E = \\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^3}$, so $E \\propto \\frac{1}{r^3}$.\nSubstituting: doubling r divides E by $2^3 = 8$, giving $E' = 160/8 = 20$ N/C.\nOption (2) assumes a $1/r^2$ law, which holds for a single charge but not for a dipole.\nSo E' = 20 N/C.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A point charge $q_1 = +2\\,\\mu$C is at the origin. A point charge $q_2 = -3\\,\\mu$C is at the point (3 m, 4 m) in vacuum. Using $\\mathbf{F}_{21} = \\frac{kq_1q_2}{r_{21}^2}\\hat{r}_{21}$, find the force $\\mathbf{F}_{21}$ on $q_2$ due to $q_1$. (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$(+1.30\\hat{i} + 1.73\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{-3}$ N","$(-1.73\\hat{i} - 1.30\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{-3}$ N","$(-1.30\\hat{i} - 1.73\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{-3}$ N","$(-6.48\\hat{i} - 8.64\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{-3}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mathbf{r}_{21} = \\mathbf{r}_2 - \\mathbf{r}_1 = 3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j}$ m, so $r_{21} = 5$ m and $\\hat{r}_{21} = 0.6\\hat{i} + 0.8\\hat{j}$.\nFormula: $\\mathbf{F}_{21} = \\frac{kq_1q_2}{r_{21}^2}\\hat{r}_{21}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{kq_1q_2}{r_{21}^2} = \\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times (2 \\times 10^{-6})(-3 \\times 10^{-6})}{25} = -2.16 \\times 10^{-3}$ N\nSo $\\mathbf{F}_{21} = -2.16 \\times 10^{-3}(0.6\\hat{i} + 0.8\\hat{j}) = (-1.30\\hat{i} - 1.73\\hat{j}) \\times 10^{-3}$ N.\nThe negative sign means the force points towards $q_1$, which is attraction. Option (4) comes from dividing by r instead of $r^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Glucose has the formula $C_6H_{12}O_6$ and molar mass 180 g/mol. Atomic numbers are C = 6, H = 1 and O = 8. Take $N_A = 6.02 \\times 10^{23}$ per mol and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. What is the total magnitude of negative charge carried by the electrons in 18 g of glucose?","image":"","options":["$9.25 \\times 10^{5}$ C","$5.78 \\times 10^{4}$ C","$9.25 \\times 10^{6}$ C","$1.54 \\times 10^{5}$ C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 18 g of glucose, so moles = $\\frac{18}{180} = 0.1$ mol.\nNumber of molecules: $0.1 \\times 6.02 \\times 10^{23} = 6.02 \\times 10^{22}$.\nElectrons per molecule: $6(6) + 12(1) + 6(8) = 96$.\nCharge: $6.02 \\times 10^{22} \\times 96 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\approx 9.25 \\times 10^{5}$ C.\nThe positive charge (protons) has the same magnitude, since the molecule is neutral.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron starts from rest and moves 2.0 cm in a uniform electric field of magnitude $1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C. How long does this take? ($e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $m_e = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg; ignore gravity)","image":"","options":["4.77 ns","3.37 ns","204 ns","47.7 ns"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $h = 0.02$ m, $E = 1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C, $m_e = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.\nFormula: $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm_e}{eE}}$, from $h = \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ with $a = \\frac{eE}{m_e}$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 0.02 \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 10^{4}}} = \\sqrt{2.275 \\times 10^{-17}}$ s.\nSo $t \\approx 4.77 \\times 10^{-9}$ s = 4.77 ns.\nOption (2) drops the factor 2. Option (3) is the result for a proton.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A neutral metal sphere acquires a charge of $+3.2\\ \\mu C$. How many electrons have been removed from it? (Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C)","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{13}$","$5 \\times 10^{12}$","$2 \\times 10^{25}$","$5 \\times 10^{-14}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 3.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ C and $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nFormula: $q = ne$, so $n = q/e$.\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{3.2 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = 2 \\times 10^{13}$.\nThe charge is positive, so these electrons were lost by the sphere.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a system of stationary point charges $q_1, q_2, q_3, \\ldots, q_n$, a student wants the force on $q_1$ due to $q_2$ alone. According to the principle of superposition, this force is:","image":"","options":["exactly what Coulomb's law gives for the pair $q_1, q_2$, unaffected by the other charges","reduced because the other charges partly shield $q_1$ from $q_2$","increased because the other charges add to the field of $q_2$","found only after subtracting the forces due to $q_3, \\ldots, q_n$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Superposition says the force between any two charges is not affected by the presence of other charges.\nSo the force on $q_1$ due to $q_2$ is given by Coulomb's law for that pair alone.\nThe other charges add their own separate forces on $q_1$. They do not modify $\\vec{F}_{12}$.\nOptions (2) and (3) wrongly assume the other charges change this pair force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (charge configuration) with List-II (how the magnitude of the electric field depends on distance $r$ from it).\nList-I:\n(A) Point charge\n(B) Electric dipole, at a point far away ($r \\gg a$)\n(C) Infinitely long uniformly charged straight wire\n(D) Uniformly charged infinite plane sheet\nList-II:\n(i) Independent of $r$\n(ii) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r}$\n(iii) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r^2}$\n(iv) $\\propto \\frac{1}{r^3}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: $E = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$, so A matches (iii).\nB: far from a dipole, $E = \\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ on the axis and $\\frac{p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ on the equator, so B matches (iv).\nC: for an infinite line, $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$, so C matches (ii).\nD: for an infinite sheet, $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$, which does not depend on distance, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical charges of $+1\\ \\mu C$ are fixed at the corners of a square. What charge must be placed at the centre of the square so that each of the five charges is in equilibrium? (Answer in $\\mu C$.)","image":"","options":["$-0.96\\ \\mu C$","$-0.25\\ \\mu C$","$-1.96\\ \\mu C$","$-0.71\\ \\mu C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: side $a$, corner charge $q = 1\\ \\mu C$, centre charge $Q$ at distance $\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}$ from each corner.\nForce on a corner from the other three charges: the two adjacent charges give $\\sqrt{2}\\frac{kq^2}{a^2}$ and the diagonal charge gives $\\frac{kq^2}{2a^2}$. Both act outward along the diagonal, so the total is $\\frac{kq^2}{a^2}\\left(\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\nThe centre charge must pull inward with $\\frac{k|Q|q}{a^2/2} = \\frac{2k|Q|q}{a^2}$.\nEquating: $|Q| = \\frac{(2\\sqrt{2} + 1)q}{4} \\approx 0.96\\ \\mu C$. It must be negative to attract. The centre charge itself feels zero net force by symmetry.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In an early atom model, a point nucleus of charge Ze sits at the centre of a uniform spherical cloud of negative charge of radius R. The atom as a whole is neutral. If $E_0 = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$, the magnitude of the electric field at $r = \\frac{R}{2}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$3.5\\,E_0$","$4.0\\,E_0$","$0.5\\,E_0$","$3.875\\,E_0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the cloud's charge density is $\\rho = -\\frac{3Ze}{4\\pi R^3}$, and the point is at $r = R/2$.\nCharge enclosed within radius r: $q = Ze - Ze\\frac{r^3}{R^3}$.\nGauss's law then gives $E = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2} - \\frac{r}{R^3}\\right)$.\nSubstituting $r = R/2$: $E = E_0\\left(4 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 3.5\\,E_0$, directed radially outward.\nThe option $4E_0$ ignores the negative charge enclosed by the Gaussian surface.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Gauss's law holds for a closed surface of any shape or size.\n(ii) A Gaussian surface may pass through a discrete point charge, provided the charge is counted as half enclosed.\n(iii) A Gaussian surface may pass through a continuous charge distribution.\n(iv) If the net flux through a closed surface is zero, the electric field must be zero at every point of that surface.\n(v) Gauss's law rests on the inverse square dependence in Coulomb's law, so any violation of it would indicate a departure from the $1/r^2$ law.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Gauss's law holds for any closed surface.\n(ii) Incorrect: the field is not well defined at a discrete charge, so the surface must not pass through one.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT allows the surface to pass through a continuous distribution.\n(iv) Incorrect: zero net flux only means zero net enclosed charge. For example, a cylinder in a uniform field has zero net flux but a non-zero field.\n(v) Correct: this is the stated link between Gauss's law and the inverse square law.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a long but finite uniformly charged straight wire, $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$ is only approximately valid, and only near the central portion of the wire.\nReason (R): Unless the wire is infinitely long, the field cannot be taken as normal to the curved surface of the cylindrical Gaussian surface with constant magnitude over it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the formula holds approximately around the central parts of a long wire, where end effects can be ignored.\nR is true. The derivation needs E to be radial and uniform on the curved surface, which requires infinite length.\nNear the ends, the field has non-radial components. This is exactly why the formula fails there, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity) with List-II (SI unit).\nList-I:\n(a) Linear charge density $\\lambda$\n(b) Surface charge density $\\sigma$\n(c) Volume charge density $\\rho$\n(d) Electric flux $\\phi$\nList-II:\n(i) C $m^{-2}$\n(ii) N $m^2$ $C^{-1}$\n(iii) C $m^{-1}$\n(iv) C $m^{-3}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\lambda = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta l}$ is charge per unit length, so its unit is C/m. This gives (a)-(iii).\n(b) $\\sigma = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta S}$ is charge per unit area, so its unit is C/$m^2$. This gives (b)-(i).\n(c) $\\rho = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta V}$ is charge per unit volume, so its unit is C/$m^3$. This gives (c)-(iv).\n(d) Flux is $E\\Delta S\\cos\\theta$, so its unit is N $C^{-1}$ $m^2$. This gives (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electric dipoles is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The total charge of an electric dipole is zero, yet its electric field is not zero.","A $CO_2$ molecule has a permanent dipole moment even in the absence of an electric field.","The dipole moment has magnitude $q \\times 2a$ and points from $-q$ to $+q$.","A water molecule is a polar molecule."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the fields of $+q$ and $-q$ do not cancel exactly because the charges are separated.\n(2) Incorrect: in $CO_2$ the centres of positive and negative charge coincide, so its permanent dipole moment is zero. It acquires a dipole moment only when a field is applied.\n(3) Correct: this is the definition, $\\vec{p} = q \\times 2a\\,\\hat{p}$.\n(4) Correct: $H_2O$ has non-coinciding charge centres.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In an early model of the atom, a point nucleus of charge $+Ze$ is surrounded by a uniform spherical distribution of negative charge $-Ze$ of radius $R$. The atom as a whole is neutral. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a distance $r = R/2$ from the nucleus, in units of $\\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$?","image":"","options":["3.5","4","4.5","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: a Gaussian sphere of radius $r < R$ encloses $q = Ze - Ze\\frac{r^3}{R^3}$.\nFormula: $E \\cdot 4\\pi r^2 = q/\\varepsilon_0$, so $E = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2} - \\frac{r}{R^3}\\right)$.\nSubstituting $r = R/2$: $E = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}\\left(4 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 3.5\\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$.\nOption (2) uses only the nucleus. Option (4) wrongly assumes the field is zero inside, as for the neutral atom outside $R$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A closed cylinder of cross-sectional area S is placed in a uniform electric field E. Its axis is parallel to the field. Face 1 is where the field lines enter, face 2 is where they leave, and surface 3 is the curved part. Using the outward-normal convention, which set of fluxes $(\\phi_1, \\phi_2, \\phi_3)$ is correct?","image":"","options":["$(-ES,\\ +ES,\\ 0)$","$(+ES,\\ +ES,\\ 0)$","$(-ES,\\ +ES,\\ 2ES)$","$(0,\\ 0,\\ 0)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Face 1: its outward normal points opposite to $\\vec{E}$, so $\\theta = 180^\\circ$ and $\\phi_1 = -ES$.\nFace 2: its outward normal points along $\\vec{E}$, so $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ and $\\phi_2 = +ES$.\nCurved surface: its normal is perpendicular to $\\vec{E}$ everywhere, so $\\phi_3 = 0$.\nThe total flux is zero, as Gauss's law requires because no charge is enclosed.\nThe tempting option (2) ignores the outward-normal sign convention. Option (4) confuses zero total flux with zero flux through each face.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Using Coulomb's law with $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$, one coulomb can be described as the charge which, when placed in vacuum at a distance of 1 m from an equal charge, experiences a repulsive force of","image":"","options":["$9 \\times 10^{9}$ N","$8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ N","1 N","$1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Put $q_1 = q_2 = 1$ C and $r = 1$ m into $F = \\frac{k q_1 q_2}{r^2}$. This gives $F = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N.\nNCERT uses this result to show that the coulomb is a very large unit. That is why $\\mu C$ and mC are used in electrostatics.\n$8.854 \\times 10^{-12}$ is the value of $\\varepsilon_0$, not a force.\n1 N would hold only if k were 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The property that differentiates the two kinds of electric charge is called the polarity of charge.\nStatement II: When a glass rod is rubbed with silk, new positive charge is created on the rod by friction.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT names the property that distinguishes positive from negative charge as polarity.\nStatement II is incorrect: rubbing creates no new charge.\nElectrons are only transferred from the rod to the silk, leaving the rod positive.\nThe number of electrons transferred is a tiny fraction of all the electrons in the rod.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the electric field lines of an isolated negative point charge?","image":"","options":["They start at the charge and go radially outward to infinity","They come in radially from infinity and end on the charge","They form closed circles around the charge","They are parallel straight lines passing by the charge"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Field lines end at negative charges.\nFor a single charge, the other end of each line is at infinity.\nThe field of a negative charge points radially inward, so the lines come from infinity and terminate on the charge.\nRadially outward lines, as in option (1), belong to a positive charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In a region of uniform electric field, the field lines are best represented by:","image":"","options":["radial lines diverging from a point","concentric circles","parallel straight lines with equal spacing","parallel straight lines whose spacing increases along the field"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A uniform field has the same direction everywhere, so the lines are parallel straight lines.\nIt also has the same magnitude everywhere, so the line density is constant and the spacing is equal.\nIncreasing spacing would mean the field gets weaker along its direction, so option (4) is not uniform.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges $+4\\,\\mu C$ and $+1\\,\\mu C$ are fixed 30 cm apart in vacuum. At what distance from the $+1\\,\\mu C$ charge, on the line joining them, is the net electric field zero?","image":"","options":["0.20 m","0.10 m","0.15 m","0.06 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: both charges are positive, so their fields oppose each other only between the charges. The null point lies between them, nearer the smaller charge.\nLet x be the distance from the 1 μC charge. Setting the fields equal: $\\frac{k(1\\,\\mu C)}{x^2} = \\frac{k(4\\,\\mu C)}{(0.3-x)^2}$.\nTaking square roots: $0.3 - x = 2x$, which gives x = 0.10 m.\nSo the null point is 0.10 m from the 1 μC charge, which is 0.20 m from the 4 μC charge.\nThe 0.20 m option is the distance from the wrong charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electric charge is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Charge, like mass, is a scalar, but unlike mass it can be negative","When a neutron turns into a proton and an electron, the total charge stays zero before and after","For a body carrying a charge of a few microcoulombs, quantisation of charge has a noticeable practical effect","When a glass rod is rubbed with silk, electrons are transferred from the rod to the silk"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: charges add algebraically like real numbers, and a charge can be positive or negative.\n(2) Correct: the proton and electron created have equal and opposite charges, so charge is conserved.\n(3) Incorrect: 1 $\\mu$C is about $10^{13}$ e. At this scale charge behaves as if it were continuous, so quantisation can be ignored.\n(4) Correct: the rod loses electrons and becomes positive; the silk gains them and becomes negative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about an electric dipole are correct?\n(i) The total charge of the dipole is zero, yet its electric field is not zero.\n(ii) At large distances, the dipole field falls off as $\\frac{1}{r^3}$.\n(iii) At large distances, the dipole field depends on $q$ and $a$ separately, not just on their product.\n(iv) For a point dipole, the formulas $\\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ (axial) and $\\frac{p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ (equatorial) are exact for every $r$.\n(v) The dipole moment vector points from $+q$ to $-q$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the fields of $+q$ and $-q$ come from different points, so they do not cancel exactly.\n(ii) Correct: the far field is proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^3}$, not $\\frac{1}{r^2}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the far field depends only on the product $qa$, which is why the dipole moment is defined.\n(iv) Correct: for a point dipole ($2a \\to 0$ with $p$ finite), these formulas are exact.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\vec{p}$ points from $-q$ to $+q$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a uniformly charged infinite plane sheet, the electric field at a point 2 cm from the sheet has the same magnitude as at a point 20 cm from it.\nStatement II: The field of a uniformly charged infinite plane sheet is $\\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$, which does not depend on the distance from the sheet.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Applying Gauss's law to a pillbox gives $2EA = \\frac{\\sigma A}{\\varepsilon_0}$, so $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}$. This result has no x-dependence.\nStatement I is correct. It follows directly from Statement II: the field is the same at every distance from an infinite sheet.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Identical charges $+q$ are placed at five of the six vertices of a regular hexagon of side $a$. The sixth vertex, F, is left empty. With $k = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}$, what is the electric field at the centre O of the hexagon?","image":"","options":["Zero, by symmetry","$\\frac{kq}{a^2}$, directed from O towards F","$\\frac{kq}{a^2}$, directed from F towards O (away from F)","$\\frac{5kq}{a^2}$, directed from O towards F"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each vertex of a regular hexagon is at distance a from the centre.\nWith all six charges present, opposite pairs cancel, so the field at O is zero.\nBy superposition, the actual field equals zero minus the field of a +q at F.\nA +q at F would produce a field of magnitude $kq/a^2$ at O, pointing away from F.\nSo the actual field is $kq/a^2$, pointing from O towards F. Option (1) ignores the missing charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A plastic comb run through dry hair attracts small bits of uncharged paper.\nReason (R): The comb's non-uniform field induces a dipole moment in the paper along the field, and a dipole whose moment is parallel to a non-uniform field feels a net force towards the region of stronger field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a comb charged by friction attracts neutral paper bits.\nR is true: the comb polarises the paper and induces a dipole moment along the field. When $\\vec{p}$ is parallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$, the net force on the dipole points towards increasing field.\nThe field of the comb is strongest near the comb, so the paper moves towards it.\nR is therefore exactly the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A very long thin straight wire carries a uniform linear charge density of $2\\ \\mu C/m$. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 30 cm from the wire, away from its ends? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 9\\times10^9$ N $m^2$ $C^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$1.2\\times10^5$ N/C","$6.0\\times10^4$ N/C","$2.4\\times10^5$ N/C","$4.0\\times10^5$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 2\\times10^{-6}$ C/m and $r = 0.3$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r} = \\frac{2k\\lambda}{r}$, where $k = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{2\\times9\\times10^9\\times2\\times10^{-6}}{0.3}$.\nSo $E = 1.2\\times10^5$ N/C.\nThe value $6.0\\times10^4$ N/C comes from wrongly using $\\frac{k\\lambda}{r}$, which drops the factor 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about electrostatic field lines are correct?\n(i) They start from positive charges and end on negative charges.\n(ii) Two field lines may cross at a point where the field is very strong.\n(iii) They can form closed loops around a charge.\n(iv) In a region of uniform field they are equally spaced parallel straight lines.\n(v) Their relative closeness indicates the relative strength of the field.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: field lines begin on positive charges and end on negative charges, or at infinity for a single charge.\n(ii) Incorrect: at a crossing point the field would have two directions, which is impossible.\n(iii) Incorrect: electrostatic field lines never form closed loops, because the field is conservative.\n(iv) Correct: a uniform field is drawn as equally spaced parallel lines.\n(v) Correct: lines crowd where the field is strong.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about electrostatic field lines are correct?\n(i) They start from positive charges and end at negative charges.\n(ii) In a charge-free region, they are continuous curves without breaks.\n(iii) Around an electric dipole, some field lines form closed loops.\n(iv) The total number of lines drawn around a charge has physical significance.\n(v) In a uniform field, they are parallel, equally spaced straight lines.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is a basic property of field lines.\n(ii) Correct: there are no breaks in a charge-free region.\n(iii) Incorrect: electrostatic field lines never form closed loops, because the electrostatic field is conservative.\n(iv) Incorrect: any number of lines may be drawn. Only the relative density of lines matters.\n(v) Correct.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field is defined as $\\vec{E} = \\lim_{q\\to 0} \\frac{\\vec{F}}{q}$, where $q$ is the test charge. Why is the limit $q \\to 0$ taken?","image":"","options":["Because the electric field depends on the magnitude of the test charge used","So that the test charge exerts negligible force on the source charge, leaving the source undisturbed while $F/q$ stays finite","Because a large test charge would change the SI unit of electric field","So that the force on the test charge becomes zero and the field also becomes zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A test charge q exerts a force on the source charge Q. This force could move Q and change the field being measured.\nMaking q negligibly small makes this disturbing force negligible.\nThe ratio F/q stays finite and defines E.\nOption (1) is wrong because E is independent of q. Option (4) is wrong because the ratio does not vanish.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A closed cylindrical surface with flat faces of area S is placed in a uniform electric field E, with its axis parallel to the field. What is the net electric flux through the whole closed surface?","image":"","options":["2ES","ES","$-ES$","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Curved surface: the outward normal is perpendicular to $\\vec{E}$, so its flux is 0.\nFace whose outward normal is along $\\vec{E}$: flux = +ES.\nFace whose outward normal is opposite to $\\vec{E}$: flux = $-ES$.\nTotal flux = ES $-$ ES + 0 = 0. This matches Gauss's law, since no charge is enclosed.\nThe option 2ES ignores the outward-normal sign convention.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation of a permanent dipole) with List-II (result).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to a uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(B) $\\vec{p}$ perpendicular to a uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(C) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$\n(D) $\\vec{p}$ antiparallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$\nList-II:\n(I) Zero torque; net force towards the region of increasing field\n(II) Zero torque; net force towards the region of decreasing field\n(III) Torque of magnitude pE; zero net force\n(IV) Zero torque and zero net force","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) In a uniform field the net force is zero, and $\\sin 0^\\circ = 0$, so the torque is also zero: IV.\n(B) In a uniform field the net force is zero, and $\\sin 90^\\circ = 1$, so the torque is maximum, pE: III.\n(C) When $\\vec{p}$ is parallel to a non-uniform $\\vec{E}$, the torque is zero and the net force points towards increasing field: I.\n(D) When $\\vec{p}$ is antiparallel, the torque is zero and the net force points towards decreasing field: II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A permanent electric dipole of moment $\\vec{p}$ is placed in an electric field $\\vec{E}$. Match List-I (situation) with List-II (result).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to $\\vec{E}$, field uniform\n(B) $\\vec{p}$ perpendicular to $\\vec{E}$, field uniform\n(C) $\\vec{p}$ parallel to $\\vec{E}$, field non-uniform\n(D) $\\vec{p}$ antiparallel to $\\vec{E}$, field non-uniform\nList-II:\n(i) Net force zero, torque of magnitude pE\n(ii) Net force zero, torque zero\n(iii) Torque zero, net force towards decreasing field\n(iv) Torque zero, net force towards increasing field","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a uniform field, the forces qE and –qE cancel, so the net force is zero. The torque is $\\tau = pE\\sin\\theta$.\n(A) $\\theta = 0$, so the torque is zero: (ii).\n(B) $\\theta = 90^\\circ$, so the torque is pE: (i).\nWhen $\\vec{p}$ is along or opposite to $\\vec{E}$, the torque is zero, but a non-uniform field gives a net force.\n(C) For $\\vec{p}$ parallel to $\\vec{E}$, the force is towards increasing field: (iv).\n(D) For $\\vec{p}$ antiparallel to $\\vec{E}$, the force is towards decreasing field: (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A closed surface of irregular shape encloses point charges $+2\\ \\mu C$ and $-5\\ \\mu C$. A third charge of $+3\\ \\mu C$ lies just outside the surface. What is the net electric flux through the closed surface? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.854\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$-3.39\\times10^{5}$ N m$^2$/C","$0$","$-5.65\\times10^{5}$ N m$^2$/C","$+1.13\\times10^{6}$ N m$^2$/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: enclosed charges are +2 μC and –5 μC. The +3 μC charge is outside the surface.\nFormula: Gauss's law, $\\phi = q_{enc}/\\varepsilon_0$. Only the enclosed charge counts, even though the outside charge does affect the field on the surface.\nSubstituting: $q_{enc} = -3\\times10^{-6}$ C, so $\\phi = -3\\times10^{-6}/8.854\\times10^{-12}$.\nSo $\\phi = -3.39\\times10^{5}$ N m$^2$/C. Option (2) wrongly includes the outside charge, giving zero.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A permanent electric dipole placed in a uniform electric field experiences zero net force.\nStatement II: A permanent electric dipole placed in a uniform electric field experiences zero torque, whatever its orientation.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The forces $q\\vec{E}$ and $-q\\vec{E}$ are equal and opposite, so the net force is zero.\nStatement II is incorrect. The two forces act at different points and form a couple: $\\tau = pE\\sin\\theta$.\nThe torque is zero only when $\\vec{p}$ is parallel or antiparallel to $\\vec{E}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges $+4\\times10^{-8}$ C and $-4\\times10^{-8}$ C are placed 0.3 m apart in vacuum. Point P is 0.3 m from each charge, so the charges and P form an equilateral triangle. What is the magnitude of the net electric field at P? (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} = 9\\times10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$4.0\\times10^{3}$ N/C, parallel to the line from the positive to the negative charge","$6.9\\times10^{3}$ N/C, parallel to the line from the positive to the negative charge","$8.0\\times10^{3}$ N/C, along the perpendicular bisector away from the charges","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $|q| = 4\\times10^{-8}$ C and r = 0.3 m from each charge.\nEach field: $E = \\frac{9\\times10^{9}\\times4\\times10^{-8}}{0.09} = 4.0\\times10^{3}$ N/C.\nThe field from +q points away from +q and the field from –q points toward –q. Each makes 60° with the line joining the charges.\nThe perpendicular components cancel. The parallel components add: $E_{net} = 2E\\cos60^\\circ = 4.0\\times10^{3}$ N/C.\nThe net field points parallel to the line from + to –.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A thin spherical shell of radius 10 cm has a uniform surface charge density $\\sigma = 8.85\\times10^{-7}$ C/m$^2$. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 30 cm from its centre? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.11\\times10^{4}$ N/C","$1.0\\times10^{5}$ N/C","$3.33\\times10^{4}$ N/C","$2.78\\times10^{3}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R = 0.1$ m, $r = 0.3$ m, $\\sigma = 8.85\\times10^{-7}$ C/m$^2$.\nFormula: for a point outside the shell, $E\\times4\\pi r^2 = \\frac{\\sigma\\times4\\pi R^2}{\\varepsilon_0}$, so $E = \\frac{\\sigma R^2}{\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{8.85\\times10^{-7}\\times0.01}{8.85\\times10^{-12}\\times0.09}$.\nSo $E \\approx 1.11\\times10^4$ N/C.\nThe option $1.0\\times10^5$ N/C is the field just at the surface, $\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The electric field due to an isolated negative point charge $-Q$ points radially outward from the charge at every point.\nStatement II: The magnitude of the electric field due to a point charge is the same at all points of a sphere centred on the charge.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A positive source charge gives a radially outward field. For a negative source charge, the field points radially inward.\nStatement II is correct. E depends only on the distance r from the charge, so it has the same magnitude everywhere on a sphere centred on the charge. This is spherical symmetry.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"By the convention introduced by Benjamin Franklin, which pair of bodies BOTH carry negative charge after the usual rubbing experiments (glass rubbed with silk, plastic rubbed with cat's fur)?","image":"","options":["Glass rod and cat's fur","Glass rod and silk","Plastic rod and silk","Plastic rod and cat's fur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"By convention, the charge on a glass rod or on cat's fur is called positive.\nThe charge on a plastic rod or on silk is called negative.\nSo the plastic rod and the silk both carry negative charge.\nOptions (2) and (4) each pair one positive body with one negative body. Option (1) pairs two positive bodies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A metal spoon held directly in the hand does not show signs of charging when rubbed, but a metal rod with a plastic handle does when rubbed without touching the metal part.\nReason (R): Charges on the metal leak to the ground through the human body, since both the metal and the body are conductors.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that a metal article held in the hand does not get electrified on rubbing, but a metal rod with an insulating handle does.\nR is true. Metals, human bodies and the earth are conductors, so any charge on the spoon flows through the body to the ground.\nR explains A: the plastic handle breaks this conducting path, so the charge stays on the rod.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In an early model of the atom, a positive point nucleus of charge $Ze$ is surrounded by a uniform spherical distribution of negative charge of radius R, and the atom as a whole is neutral. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The electric field is zero for all $r > R$.\n(ii) The electric field at $r = R$ is zero.\n(iii) At $r = R/2$, the field is $\\frac{7}{8}$ of the field the nucleus alone would produce at that point.\n(iv) For $r < R$, the field is directed radially inward.\n(v) The magnitude of the negative charge density is $\\frac{3Ze}{4\\pi R^3}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a sphere with $r > R$ encloses zero net charge, so E = 0.\n(ii) Correct: inside, $E = \\frac{Ze}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2} - \\frac{r}{R^3}\\right)$, which is 0 at $r = R$.\n(iii) Correct: at $r = R/2$, the bracket is $\\frac{4}{R^2} - \\frac{1}{2R^2} = \\frac{3.5}{R^2}$. The nucleus alone gives $\\frac{4}{R^2}$, and the ratio is $\\frac{7}{8}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the enclosed charge is positive, so the field points outward.\n(v) Correct: $Ze = |\\rho|\\cdot\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two small charged metal spheres A and B, held on insulating supports, repel each other with a force of 0.40 N when their centres are 12 cm apart. Sphere A alone is touched with an identical uncharged sphere C, and C is removed. B is then moved so that the centre-to-centre distance becomes 6 cm. Sizes are negligible compared with the separation. What is the new force between A and B?","image":"","options":["0.20 N","0.40 N","1.60 N","0.80 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: F = 0.40 N at r = 12 cm.\nAfter touching C, A's charge becomes $\\frac{q}{2}$. B keeps $q'$.\nFormula: $F' = \\frac{k (q/2) q'}{(r/2)^2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times F = 2F$\nSubstituting: $F' = 2 \\times 0.40$\nSo F' = 0.80 N. If both spheres had been halved, as in NCERT Example 1.4, the force would have stayed 0.40 N.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following molecules has a permanent electric dipole moment even when no external electric field is applied?","image":"","options":["$CO_2$","$CH_4$","$H_2O$","$H_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A polar molecule is one in which the centres of positive and negative charge do not coincide. Such a molecule has a permanent dipole moment.\nNCERT gives $H_2O$ as the example of a polar molecule.\nIn $CO_2$ and $CH_4$ the two charge centres coincide, so their dipole moment is zero. They develop a dipole moment only when a field is applied.\n$H_2$ is a symmetric molecule and is also non-polar.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Take $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. How many of the following values can be the net charge on an isolated body?\n$1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $2.4 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $8.0 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $3.2 \\times 10^{-20}$ C, $-4.8 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: a free charge must be $q = ne$, where n is an integer.\n$1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C: n = 1, so it is allowed.\n$2.4 \\times 10^{-19}$ C: n = 1.5, so it is not allowed.\n$8.0 \\times 10^{-19}$ C: n = 5, so it is allowed.\n$3.2 \\times 10^{-20}$ C: n = 0.2, so it is not allowed.\n$-4.8 \\times 10^{-19}$ C: n = −3, so it is allowed. Negative integers are permitted.\nThree values are possible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A short electric dipole produces an electric field of magnitude $4.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C at a point on its equatorial plane 20 cm from its centre. What is the magnitude of its field at a point on its axis 40 cm from its centre?","image":"","options":["$8.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","$5.0 \\times 10^{3}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $E_{eq} = 4.0 \\times 10^4$ N/C at $r_1 = 0.20$ m. We need $E_{ax}$ at $r_2 = 0.40$ m.\nFormula: $E_{eq} = \\frac{p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$ and $E_{ax} = \\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^3}$.\nTaking the ratio: $E_{ax} = E_{eq} \\times 2 \\times \\left(\\frac{r_1}{r_2}\\right)^3 = 4.0 \\times 10^4 \\times 2 \\times \\frac{1}{8}$.\nSo $E_{ax} = 1.0 \\times 10^4$ N/C.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Glass rods are rubbed with silk and plastic rods are rubbed with cat's fur. How many of the following observations are correct?\n(i) Two such glass rods repel each other.\n(ii) A plastic rod attracts a glass rod.\n(iii) The glass rod repels the cat's fur.\n(iv) The plastic rod attracts the silk used to rub the glass rod.\n(v) The glass rod attracts the silk with which it was rubbed.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Glass and fur are positive; plastic and silk are negative.\n(i) Correct: like charges (+, +) repel.\n(ii) Correct: unlike charges (−, +) attract.\n(iii) Correct: glass (+) and fur (+) repel.\n(iv) Incorrect: plastic (−) and silk (−) repel.\n(v) Correct: glass (+) and silk (−) attract.\nFour observations are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The macroscopic surface charge density $\\sigma$ defined on a charged conductor ignores the quantisation of charge.\nReason (R): $\\sigma$ is defined as $\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta S}$ for an area element that is small on the macroscopic scale but contains a very large number of microscopic charged constituents, so $\\sigma$ is a smoothed-out average.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says $\\sigma$ ignores the quantisation of charge and the discontinuity of charge at the microscopic level.\nR is true. $\\Delta S$ is macroscopically small but microscopically large, so it contains many electrons.\nR explains A. Because $\\sigma$ averages over a huge number of discrete charges, the steps of size $e$ are lost and $\\sigma$ behaves as a continuous function.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+q$ at A, $+q$ at B and $-q$ at C are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side $l$. Let $F = \\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}$. Consider the statements:\n(i) The net force on the charge at A has magnitude $F$ and is parallel to BC (directed from B towards C).\n(ii) The net force on the charge at A is along CA, directed away from C.\n(iii) The net force on the charge at C has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}F$, along the bisector of angle ACB, pointing towards side AB.\n(iv) The vector sum of the net forces on the three charges is zero.\n(v) The net force on the charge at B has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}F$.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (iv) and (v) only","(ii) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At A: B repels along BA and C attracts along AC, each with magnitude $F$. The two forces are $120^\\circ$ apart, so the resultant is $F$ along BC. Hence (i) is true and (ii) is false.\nAt B: by the same reasoning, the resultant is $F$ along AC, not $\\sqrt{3}F$. Hence (v) is false.\nAt C: A and B each attract with magnitude $F$, $60^\\circ$ apart. The resultant is $2F\\cos 30^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}F$ along the bisector, towards AB. Hence (iii) is true.\n(iv) is true: Coulomb forces obey Newton's third law, so the internal forces sum to zero.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the electrostatic force between point charges varied as $1/r^3$ instead of $1/r^2$, the flux of a point charge through a concentric spherical surface would still be the same for every radius.\nStatement II: The experimental finding that the field is zero everywhere inside a uniformly charged thin spherical shell confirms the $1/r^2$ dependence in Coulomb's law.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: With E $\\propto 1/r^3$, the flux through the sphere is E $\\times 4\\pi r^2 \\propto 1/r$. It would change with radius, so Statement I is incorrect.\nRadius independence, and hence Gauss's law, depends on the exact inverse-square law.\nStatement II: The result E = 0 inside the shell follows from Gauss's law. NCERT notes that verifying it experimentally confirms the $1/r^2$ law, so Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a glass rod is rubbed with silk, some electrons move from the rod to the silk, so the rod becomes positively charged.\nStatement II: During rubbing, new negative charge is created on the silk, which is why the silk becomes negatively charged.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Loosely bound electrons transfer from the glass to the silk. The glass loses electrons and becomes positive.\nStatement II is incorrect. No new charge is created by rubbing. The silk is negative only because it gains the electrons the rod lost, and total charge is conserved.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An isolated system contains five point charges: $+2\\ \\mu C$, $-5\\ \\mu C$, $+7\\ \\mu C$, $-3\\ \\mu C$ and $-4\\ \\mu C$. What is the total charge of the system?","image":"","options":["$-3\\ \\mu C$","$+3\\ \\mu C$","$+9\\ \\mu C$","$+21\\ \\mu C$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: five charges with their signs.\nRule: charges are scalars, so they add algebraically with proper signs (additivity).\nSubstituting: $(+2) + (-5) + (+7) + (-3) + (-4) = 9 - 12 = -3\\ \\mu C$.\nSo the total charge is $-3\\ \\mu C$.\nOption (4), $+21\\ \\mu C$, wrongly adds magnitudes and ignores signs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two small charged metal spheres A and B repel each other with a force of 12 mN when their centres are a distance $r$ apart. Sphere A is touched once by an identical uncharged sphere, which is then removed. Sphere B is touched one after another by two identical uncharged spheres, each removed after contact. The separation between A and B is then made $r/2$. What is the new force between A and B? (Ignore the sizes of the spheres compared with their separation.)","image":"","options":["6 mN","12 mN","24 mN","3 mN"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $F = \\frac{kqq'}{r^2} = 12$ mN.\nOne touch by an identical uncharged sphere halves A's charge to $q/2$.\nTwo successive touches reduce B's charge to $q'/2$ and then $q'/4$.\nFormula: $F' = \\frac{k(q/2)(q'/4)}{(r/2)^2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{kqq'}{r^2} = \\frac{F}{2}$.\nSo $F' = 6$ mN.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A charge $+q$ is fixed at $x = 0$ and a charge $-4q$ is fixed at $x = L$ on the x-axis. At which point on the x-axis would a third point charge experience zero net electrostatic force?","image":"","options":["Between the charges, at $x = \\frac{L}{3}$","Between the charges, at $x = \\frac{2L}{3}$","Beyond the $-4q$ charge, at $x = 2L$","Beyond the $+q$ charge, at $x = -L$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Between unlike charges, the forces on a test charge point the same way, so the force cannot vanish there. This rules out options (1) and (2).\nThe null point must lie outside, nearer the smaller charge $+q$, i.e. at $x = -d$ with $d > 0$.\nFor the forces to balance: $\\frac{q}{d^2} = \\frac{4q}{(d+L)^2}$, which gives $d + L = 2d$, so $d = L$.\nAt $x = 2L$ (option 3), the $-4q$ charge is both nearer and larger, so its force dominates and cannot be balanced.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Two point charges of $+2\\ \\mu C$ and $+3\\ \\mu C$ are placed 30 cm apart in vacuum. What is the magnitude of the electrostatic force between them? (Take $k = 9 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.18 N","1.8 N","0.6 N","6.0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $q_1 = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ C, $q_2 = 3 \\times 10^{-6}$ C, $r = 0.30$ m.\nFormula: $F = \\frac{k q_1 q_2}{r^2}$\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 6 \\times 10^{-12}}{0.09}$\nSo F = 0.6 N. The force is repulsive because both charges are positive.\nUsing r = 30 instead of 0.30 m, or forgetting to square r, gives the wrong options.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron falls from rest through height $h$ in a uniform field of magnitude $E$. The field is then reversed (same magnitude), and a proton falls from rest through the same height $h$. Gravity is neglected.\nStatement I: Both particles gain the same kinetic energy.\nStatement II: At the end of the fall, the electron has a larger momentum than the proton.","image":"","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: each particle feels a force of magnitude $eE$ over the same distance $h$. The work done, and so the kinetic energy gained, is $K = eEh$ for both. True.\nStatement II: momentum $p = \\sqrt{2mK}$. With equal $K$, the heavier proton has the larger momentum, about $\\sqrt{1833} \\approx 43$ times that of the electron. False.\nThe electron does have the larger speed, which is what makes Statement II tempting.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A charge $+4 \\times 10^{-8}$ C is fixed at x = 0. A charge $-1 \\times 10^{-8}$ C is fixed at x = 0.1 m on the x-axis. At what position on the x-axis (other than at infinity) is the net electric field zero?","image":"","options":["x = 0.05 m","x = 0.0667 m","x = 0.2 m","x = 0.3 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Between the charges, both fields point in the +x direction (away from + and towards −). So they add and cannot cancel. This rules out 0.05 m and 0.0667 m.\nFor x < 0, the point is nearer the larger charge, so the fields cannot cancel there either.\nFor x > 0.1 m, the fields are opposite. Setting their magnitudes equal: $\\frac{4}{x^2} = \\frac{1}{(x-0.1)^2}$\nThis gives $2(x - 0.1) = x$, so x = 0.2 m.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+5\\,\\mu C$ and $-5\\,\\mu C$ are placed 2 cm apart. Using the exact expression (not the short-dipole approximation), find the electric field at a point on the axis 5 cm from the centre of the dipole, on the side of the positive charge. (Take $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} = 9 \\times 10^9$ N m$^2$ C$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.56 \\times 10^{7}$ N/C","$1.44 \\times 10^{7}$ N/C","$7.2 \\times 10^{6}$ N/C","$1.25 \\times 10^{7}$ N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $q = 5 \\times 10^{-6}$ C, $a = 0.01$ m, $r = 0.05$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\cdot \\frac{4ar}{(r^2 - a^2)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $4ar = 2 \\times 10^{-3}$ and $(r^2 - a^2)^2 = (0.0024)^2 = 5.76 \\times 10^{-6}$.\nSo $E = 4.5 \\times 10^4 \\times 347.2 \\approx 1.56 \\times 10^7$ N/C, along $\\vec{p}$.\nThe short-dipole formula $\\frac{2p}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^3}$ gives $1.44 \\times 10^7$ N/C, option (2). It is invalid here because r is not much larger than a.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A dipole placed with its moment $\\vec{p}$ parallel to a non-uniform electric field experiences a net force towards the region of stronger field.\nReason (R): When $\\vec{p}$ is parallel to $\\vec{E}$, the torque on the dipole is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In a non-uniform field, the force on $+q$ and the force on $-q$ have unequal magnitudes. With $\\vec{p}$ parallel to $\\vec{E}$, the net force is in the direction of increasing field. This explains why a polarised piece of paper moves towards a charged comb.\nR is true. $\\tau = pE\\sin 0^\\circ = 0$.\nHowever, zero torque says nothing about the net force. The force arises from the non-uniformity of the field, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Four equal charges $+q$ are fixed at the corners of a square of side $a$. A charge $Q$ is placed at the centre of the square. For each corner charge to experience zero net force, $Q$ must be:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{q}{4}$","$-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}q$","$-\\frac{(1+2\\sqrt{2})}{2}q$","$-\\frac{(1+2\\sqrt{2})}{4}q$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: corner charge $q$, side $a$, centre at distance $\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}$ from each corner.\nForces from the other corners (superposition): the two adjacent charges give $\\frac{kq^2}{a^2}$ each, at right angles, so their resultant is $\\sqrt{2}\\frac{kq^2}{a^2}$ along the diagonal. The opposite corner gives $\\frac{kq^2}{2a^2}$ along the same diagonal. Total outward force: $\\frac{kq^2}{a^2}(\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{2})$.\nThe force due to $Q$ is $\\frac{2kq|Q|}{a^2}$. It must point inward, so $Q$ is negative.\nEquating: $2|Q| = (\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{2})q$.\nSo $Q = -\\frac{(1+2\\sqrt{2})}{4}q \\approx -0.96q$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"Charges $+q$, $+q$ and $-q$ are placed at vertices A, B and C respectively of an equilateral triangle. Let $F$ be the magnitude of the Coulomb force between any one pair. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The net force on the charge at A has magnitude $F$.\n(ii) The net force on the charge at A is parallel to BC.\n(iii) The net force on the charge at C has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}F$ and lies along the bisector of angle BCA.\n(iv) The vector sum of the net forces on all three charges is zero.\n(v) The net force on the charge at C points away from side AB.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the force from B (along BA) and the force from C (along AC) are equal and $120^\\circ$ apart, so the resultant has magnitude $F$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\vec{BA} + \\vec{AC} = \\vec{BC}$, so the resultant is along BC.\n(iii) Correct: the two attractive forces are $60^\\circ$ apart, giving $\\sqrt{3}F$ along the bisector.\n(iv) Correct: this follows from Newton's third law, since the internal forces cancel in pairs.\n(v) Incorrect: C is attracted towards A and B, so the net force points towards AB.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT Example 1.7, an electron and a proton fall in a uniform electric field of $2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C. Gravity is ignored when finding their times of fall. Why is this justified?","image":"","options":["The electric acceleration (of the order of $10^{12}$ m/s$^2$ or more) is enormous compared with $g \\approx 10$ m/s$^2$","Gravity does not act on charged particles","The electric force and gravity cancel each other exactly","Gravity acts only on neutral bodies placed in a field"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For the proton, $a_p = \\frac{eE}{m_p} \\approx 1.9 \\times 10^{12}$ m/s$^2$. The electron's acceleration is even larger.\nThis is about $10^{11}$ times $g$, so adding $g$ changes the result negligibly.\nGravity does act on charged particles, so option (2) is wrong. Nothing cancels, so option (3) is wrong. Gravity is simply negligible here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"A flat surface of area 0.02 m$^2$ is placed in a uniform electric field of magnitude 500 N C$^{-1}$. The normal to the surface makes an angle of 60° with the field. What is the electric flux through the surface?","image":"","options":["5 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","8.66 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","10 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$","0 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: E = 500 N C$^{-1}$, $\\Delta S$ = 0.02 m$^2$, $\\theta$ = 60° between $\\vec{E}$ and the area vector (the normal).\nFormula: $\\phi = E\\Delta S\\cos\\theta$.\nSubstituting: $\\phi = 500 \\times 0.02 \\times \\cos 60° = 10 \\times 0.5$.\nSo $\\phi$ = 5 N m$^2$ C$^{-1}$. The value 8.66 comes from wrongly using $\\sin\\theta$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-CHARGES-FIELDS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electric Charges and Fields","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electric Charges and Fields","type":"mcq","question":"An electron starting from rest falls through a height $h$ in a uniform field of $2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C. What field magnitude must act on a proton (field direction chosen to push it downward) so that it falls through the same height in the same time? ($m_p = 1.67 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg, $m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg; ignore gravity)","image":"","options":["$3.67 \\times 10^{7}$ N/C","$8.56 \\times 10^{5}$ N/C","$2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N/C","10.9 N/C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2hm}{eE}}$, with the same $h$ and the same charge magnitude $e$ for both particles.\nCondition: equal times require $\\frac{m_e}{E_e} = \\frac{m_p}{E_p}$, so $E_p = E_e \\frac{m_p}{m_e}$.\nSubstituting: $E_p = 2.0 \\times 10^{4} \\times \\frac{1.67 \\times 10^{-27}}{9.11 \\times 10^{-31}} = 2.0 \\times 10^{4} \\times 1833$.\nSo $E_p \\approx 3.67 \\times 10^{7}$ N/C.\nOption (2) wrongly scales by $\\sqrt{m_p/m_e}$. The masses must have equal accelerations, so the field scales with the full mass ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 1 · Electric Charges and Fields","difficulty":"hard"}]});
